window.PLAZA_FIGURES = [{"id":"osiel_cardenas_guillen","name":"Osiel Cárdenas Guillén","alias":"El Mata Amigos / El Loco","cartels":["gulf","zetas"],"born":1967,"died":0,"role":"Gulf Cartel boss who created Los Zetas as his private army","status_2026":"Released from US prison 30 Aug 2024, deported to Mexico 16 Dec 2024 and detained on arrival; held in Mexico facing domestic charges","bio":"Born 18 May 1967 in Matamoros, Tamaulipas, Cárdenas worked as a mechanic and briefly as a federal judicial police officer before entering the Gulf Cartel. In 1999 he had his partner Salvador Gómez killed, earning the alias 'El Mata Amigos' (Friend-Killer), and took control of the organisation. Fearing rivals and the state, he hired Arturo Guzmán Decena, a deserter from the army's GAFE special forces, to recruit fellow commandos; the resulting armed wing, Los Zetas, became the template for cartel paramilitarism across Mexico. On 9 November 1999 he surrounded and threatened FBI and DEA agents on a Matamoros street, an incident that made him a US priority target. Mexican soldiers captured him after a firefight in Matamoros on 14 March 2003. He continued directing the cartel from prison until his extradition to the United States on 19 January 2007. He pleaded guilty in Houston and on 24 February 2010 received 25 years and a $50 million forfeiture. He completed the term at USP Terre Haute, was released to ICE custody on 30 August 2024, and was deported to Mexico on 16 December 2024, where he was detained immediately.","notable":["Founded Los Zetas around 1999 by recruiting GAFE army deserters","Threatened US FBI and DEA agents at gunpoint in Matamoros, November 1999","Ran the Gulf Cartel from Mexican prison between 2003 and 2007","Sentenced in Houston to 25 years and $50 million forfeiture, February 2010","Deported back to Mexico and re-arrested in December 2024"],"state":"TAM","lat":25.8797,"lng":-97.5042,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Osiel_C%C3%A1rdenas_Guill%C3%A9n","https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Osiel_C%C3%A1rdenas_Guill%C3%A9n","https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/"],"verified":false},{"id":"antonio_ezequiel_cardenas_guillen","name":"Antonio Ezequiel Cárdenas Guillén","alias":"Tony Tormenta","cartels":["gulf"],"born":1962,"died":2010,"role":"Co-leader of the Gulf Cartel after his brother Osiel's extradition","status_2026":"Killed 5 November 2010 in Matamoros, Tamaulipas","bio":"Born 5 March 1962 in Matamoros, Tamaulipas, Antonio Ezequiel Cárdenas Guillén was the younger brother of Gulf Cartel boss Osiel Cárdenas. He acquired the nickname 'Tony Tormenta' (Tony Storm) for his volatility. After Osiel's 2003 capture and 2007 extradition to the United States, he shared command of the Gulf Cartel with Jorge Eduardo 'El Coss' Costilla Sánchez, with Costilla handling strategy and political protection while Cárdenas represented the family's interests and controlled the lucrative Matamoros plaza on the Rio Grande. He was indicted in the United States on drug trafficking and money laundering counts and carried a multi-million dollar US reward. In early 2010 the Gulf Cartel broke violently with its former armed wing, Los Zetas, plunging Tamaulipas into open warfare and prompting a heavy federal deployment. On 5 November 2010, Mexican marines cornered him in Matamoros. More than 660 marines, seventeen vehicles and three helicopters were committed to an eight-hour battle that shut down the border city; Cárdenas was killed, along with several of his gunmen, two marines and a journalist. The official death toll was disputed, with independent accounts putting casualties far higher.","notable":["Shared Gulf Cartel command with 'El Coss' from 2007 to 2010","Controlled the Matamoros border crossing into Brownsville, Texas","Fought the 2010 Gulf Cartel war against Los Zetas","Killed 5 November 2010 in an eight-hour battle involving over 660 marines","US authorities had offered a $5 million reward for his capture"],"state":"TAM","lat":25.8797,"lng":-97.5042,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antonio_Ezequiel_C%C3%A1rdenas_Guill%C3%A9n","https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/"],"verified":false},{"id":"jorge_eduardo_costilla_sanchez","name":"Jorge Eduardo Costilla Sánchez","alias":"El Coss","cartels":["gulf"],"born":1971,"died":0,"role":"Gulf Cartel leader who broke the alliance with Los Zetas in 2010","status_2026":"Imprisoned in the United States, life sentence imposed 22 September 2022","bio":"Born 6 January 1971 in Matamoros, Tamaulipas, Costilla Sánchez served as a municipal police officer before joining the Gulf Cartel, rising through its ranks as a protégé of Osiel Cárdenas. He was present during the November 1999 confrontation in which Cárdenas menaced US federal agents. When Cárdenas was jailed in 2003, Costilla assumed day-to-day command, later sharing power with Antonio Ezequiel 'Tony Tormenta' Cárdenas after the boss's 2007 extradition. Costilla specialised in bribery networks and political protection rather than battlefield command. In early 2010 he presided over the rupture between the Gulf Cartel and Los Zetas, its former enforcement wing, after Zetas gunmen killed a Gulf lieutenant and refused to hand over the culprits; the split ignited a war that made Tamaulipas and Nuevo León among the deadliest states in Mexico. With Tony Tormenta dead in November 2010, Costilla became sole leader. Mexican marines arrested him without a shot fired in Tampico, Tamaulipas, on 12 September 2012. He was transferred to the United States on 30 September 2015, pleaded guilty on 26 September 2017 to cocaine and marijuana trafficking and two counts of assaulting federal agents, and on 22 September 2022 a federal court in Brownsville, Texas sentenced him to life imprisonment with a $5 million forfeiture.","notable":["Former municipal police officer who rose to lead the Gulf Cartel","Took part in the 1999 armed standoff with US agents in Matamoros","Presided over the 2010 Gulf Cartel split from Los Zetas","Captured without gunfire in Tampico, 12 September 2012","Sentenced to life in Brownsville, Texas, 22 September 2022"],"state":"TAM","lat":25.8797,"lng":-97.5042,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jorge_Eduardo_Costilla_S%C3%A1nchez","https://www.bop.gov/inmateloc/","https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/"],"verified":false},{"id":"arturo_guzman_decena","name":"Arturo Guzmán Decena","alias":"Z-1","cartels":["zetas","gulf"],"born":1976,"died":2002,"role":"Army special forces deserter who founded Los Zetas","status_2026":"Killed 21 November 2002 in Matamoros, Tamaulipas","bio":"Born 13 January 1976 in Puebla, Guzmán Decena joined the Mexican Army and was selected for the Grupo Aeromóvil de Fuerzas Especiales (GAFE), an elite airmobile special forces unit trained in counter-insurgency, some of it by foreign instructors. He served in operations against the Zapatista insurgency in Chiapas in 1994. He deserted in 1997 and went to work for Osiel Cárdenas Guillén and the Gulf Cartel in Tamaulipas. Asked by Cárdenas to assemble the best armed men available, Guzmán Decena recruited roughly thirty to forty fellow deserters from GAFE and other army units, luring them with pay far above military wages. The unit took its name from the radio call sign 'Zeta'; as its commander he became Z-1 and assigned sequential Z numbers to his recruits. Los Zetas introduced military tactics, disciplined command structures, heavy weapons and systematic terror into Mexican drug trafficking, transforming cartel violence for a generation. Guzmán Decena was killed on 21 November 2002 when Mexican soldiers surprised him inside a restaurant in Matamoros, Tamaulipas. A wreath left at his grave reportedly read that he was the boss of bosses of the special forces.","notable":["GAFE special forces officer who deserted the army in 1997","Recruited roughly 30 to 40 military deserters to form Los Zetas","Originated the 'Z' numbering system used by the organisation","Introduced military doctrine and heavy weapons to cartel warfare","Shot dead by soldiers in a Matamoros restaurant, 21 November 2002"],"state":"TAM","lat":25.8797,"lng":-97.5042,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arturo_Guzm%C3%A1n_Decena","https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/"],"verified":false},{"id":"heriberto_lazcano","name":"Heriberto Lazcano Lazcano","alias":"Z-3 / El Verdugo / El Lazca","cartels":["zetas","gulf"],"born":1974,"died":2012,"role":"Supreme leader of Los Zetas from 2002 until 2012","status_2026":"Killed 7 October 2012 in Progreso, Coahuila; his corpse was stolen from a funeral home","bio":"Born 25 December 1974 in Apan, Hidalgo, Lazcano enlisted in the Mexican Army at sixteen and was admitted to the GAFE special forces, receiving counter-insurgency and combat training. He deserted in the late 1990s and joined Arturo Guzmán Decena's founding cadre of Los Zetas, taking the call sign Z-3 and the nickname 'El Verdugo' (The Executioner) for his personal use of torture. He assumed command after Guzmán Decena's death in November 2002. Under Lazcano, Los Zetas expanded from a Gulf Cartel enforcement wing into an autonomous criminal enterprise controlling territory in Coahuila, Nuevo León, Veracruz, Tabasco and into Guatemala, and diversifying into extortion, kidnapping, fuel theft and migrant smuggling. In early 2010 the Zetas formally broke with the Gulf Cartel, triggering a war across the northeast. His organisation was implicated in the August 2010 San Fernando massacre of 72 migrants and the 2011 Allende, Coahuila killings. On 7 October 2012 Mexican marines killed him in a shootout in Progreso, Coahuila; before authorities could complete forensic confirmation, armed men seized his body from a funeral home in nearby Sabinas, fuelling years of doubt about his death.","notable":["GAFE-trained soldier who became the third Zeta, Z-3","Led Los Zetas from November 2002 until October 2012","Turned the group from a Gulf enforcement wing into an independent cartel","Presided over the 2010 split with the Gulf Cartel and the San Fernando massacres","Body stolen from a Sabinas funeral home days after he was killed"],"state":"TAM","lat":27.4761,"lng":-99.5164,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heriberto_Lazcano","https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/"],"verified":false},{"id":"miguel_angel_trevino_morales","name":"Miguel Ángel Treviño Morales","alias":"Z-40","cartels":["zetas","gulf","cdn"],"born":1970,"died":0,"role":"Los Zetas leader notorious for extreme violence and mass killings","status_2026":"In US federal custody after transfer from Mexico on 27 February 2025; awaiting trial","bio":"Born 18 November 1970 in Nuevo Laredo, Tamaulipas, Treviño Morales grew up partly in Texas and began as a runner and enforcer for the Gulf Cartel in Nuevo Laredo before joining Los Zetas around 2005, taking the call sign Z-40. He led the Zetas' brutal campaign to seize Nuevo Laredo from the Sinaloa Cartel and became second-in-command to Heriberto Lazcano, running the group's most violent operations. He is linked to the August 2010 San Fernando massacre of 72 Central and South American migrants, the 2011 discovery of nearly 200 bodies in mass graves in the same municipality, and the March 2011 destruction of Allende, Coahuila, where dozens of residents were abducted and killed in reprisal for a DEA informant leak. He was also known for the practice of burning victims in oil drums. After Lazcano was killed in October 2012, Treviño became supreme leader. Mexican marines captured him without a shot near Nuevo Laredo on 15 July 2013. He remained imprisoned in Mexico until 27 February 2025, when Mexico handed him and 28 other capos to US authorities amid tariff pressure from Washington; he faces federal drug trafficking, money laundering and murder charges.","notable":["Rose from Nuevo Laredo street enforcer to supreme leader of Los Zetas","Linked to the 2010 San Fernando massacre of 72 migrants","Ordered the March 2011 Allende, Coahuila massacre after a DEA leak","Captured by Mexican marines near Nuevo Laredo, 15 July 2013","Sent to US custody with 28 other capos on 27 February 2025"],"state":"TAM","lat":27.4761,"lng":-99.5164,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miguel_Trevi%C3%B1o_Morales","https://insightcrime.org/criminal-profiles/","https://www.bop.gov/inmateloc/"],"verified":false},{"id":"omar_trevino_morales","name":"Omar Treviño Morales","alias":"Z-42","cartels":["zetas","cdn"],"born":1974,"died":0,"role":"Last supreme leader of Los Zetas before the group fragmented","status_2026":"In US federal custody after transfer from Mexico on 27 February 2025; awaiting trial","bio":"Born 26 January 1974 in Nuevo Laredo, Tamaulipas, Omar Treviño Morales followed his older brother Miguel Ángel into the Gulf Cartel and then Los Zetas, taking the call sign Z-42. He oversaw operations in Coahuila and Nuevo León and was indicted in Washington, D.C. in 2008 on drug trafficking charges; the United States offered a $5 million reward for his capture, and the Treasury Department sanctioned him under the Foreign Narcotics Kingpin Designation Act in 2010. When Miguel was captured in July 2013, Omar inherited a weakened and fracturing organisation. Under his brief leadership Los Zetas splintered, with the Treviño family's own faction eventually reconstituting itself as the Cártel del Noreste, based in Nuevo Laredo, while a rival wing became the Zetas Vieja Escuela. Mexican federal police and army units arrested him without firing a shot at a house in San Pedro Garza García, an affluent suburb of Monterrey, Nuevo León, on 4 March 2015. He was charged in Mexico with money laundering and weapons offences and held in maximum security. On 27 February 2025 he was transferred to the United States alongside his brother and 27 other figures.","notable":["Took over Los Zetas after his brother Miguel's capture in July 2013","Presided over the fragmentation of Los Zetas into CDN and Vieja Escuela","Arrested in San Pedro Garza García, Nuevo León, 4 March 2015","US reward of $5 million and Kingpin Act sanctions in 2010","Transferred to US custody on 27 February 2025"],"state":"TAM","lat":27.4761,"lng":-99.5164,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Omar_Trevi%C3%B1o_Morales","https://insightcrime.org/criminal-profiles/"],"verified":false},{"id":"arturo_beltran_leyva","name":"Marcos Arturo Beltrán Leyva","alias":"El Barbas / Jefe de Jefes","cartels":["beltran_leyva","sinaloa"],"born":1961,"died":2009,"role":"Founder and boss of the Beltrán Leyva Organization","status_2026":"Killed 16 December 2009 in Cuernavaca, Morelos","bio":"Born 27 September 1961 in Badiraguato, Sinaloa, Arturo Beltrán Leyva was the eldest of four brothers who rose within the Sinaloa Federation as close allies of Joaquín 'El Chapo' Guzmán and Ismael 'El Mayo' Zambada. He ran the Federation's corruption apparatus, cultivating police and political protection at the highest levels, and controlled trafficking corridors through Guerrero, Morelos, Nayarit and Sonora. The alliance collapsed after his brother Alfredo was arrested in Culiacán on 21 January 2008; Arturo blamed El Chapo for betraying him to the authorities. The Beltrán Leyva Organization broke away and allied with Los Zetas against Sinaloa, unleashing a war that devastated Sinaloa, Guerrero and Morelos and pioneered the display of decapitated bodies and hanging of banners. He recruited Edgar 'La Barbie' Valdez Villarreal and Sergio 'El Grande' Villarreal Barragán as enforcers. On 16 December 2009 roughly 200 Mexican marines besieged his luxury apartment complex in Cuernavaca, Morelos; after a ninety-minute firefight he and three gunmen were killed. Marine Melquisedec Angulo Córdova died in the raid, and gunmen murdered members of his family days after his funeral in a notorious act of reprisal.","notable":["Ran the Sinaloa Federation's police and political corruption network","Broke with El Chapo in 2008 after his brother Alfredo's arrest","Allied the BLO with Los Zetas against the Sinaloa Cartel","Killed by 200 marines in Cuernavaca, 16 December 2009","His organisation murdered the family of the marine killed in the raid"],"state":"MOR","lat":18.9186,"lng":-99.2342,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arturo_Beltr%C3%A1n_Leyva","https://insightcrime.org/criminal-profiles/"],"verified":false},{"id":"alfredo_beltran_leyva","name":"Alfredo Beltrán Leyva","alias":"El Mochomo","cartels":["beltran_leyva","sinaloa"],"born":1971,"died":0,"role":"Beltrán Leyva brother whose 2008 arrest split the Sinaloa Federation","status_2026":"Imprisoned in the US, life sentence, ADX Florence, Colorado","bio":"Born 21 January 1971 in Badiraguato, Sinaloa, Alfredo Beltrán Leyva was nicknamed 'El Mochomo' after a desert ant. With his brothers Arturo, Héctor and Carlos he managed cocaine and methamphetamine trafficking for the Sinaloa Federation, overseeing shipments through Sinaloa and Sonora into Arizona and California, and handled much of the organisation's day-to-day logistics and bribery of state police. Mexican Army special forces arrested him in Culiacán, Sinaloa, on 21 January 2008 along with bodyguards, cash and weapons. His brother Arturo concluded that Joaquín 'El Chapo' Guzmán had informed on him in exchange for protection for his own son, and the Beltrán Leyva family split from the Federation. The rupture set off one of the bloodiest phases of the drug war, with Culiacán, Guerrero and Morelos turned into battlegrounds. Alfredo was extradited to the United States on 15 November 2014, pleaded guilty before US District Judge Richard J. Leon on 23 February 2016, and was sentenced on 5 April 2017 to life imprisonment without parole and a $529 million forfeiture, calculated on at least 27.9 tons of narcotics shipped between 2000 and 2012. He was moved to the ADX Florence supermax in June 2022.","notable":["Arrest in Culiacán on 21 January 2008 triggered the Sinaloa-BLO war","Extradited to the United States on 15 November 2014","Pleaded guilty in Washington, D.C. in February 2016","Sentenced to life without parole and $529 million forfeiture in 2017","Transferred to ADX Florence supermax in June 2022"],"state":"SIN","lat":24.7993,"lng":-107.3938,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alfredo_Beltr%C3%A1n_Leyva","https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alfredo_Beltr%C3%A1n_Leyva","https://www.bop.gov/inmateloc/"],"verified":false},{"id":"hector_beltran_leyva","name":"Héctor Beltrán Leyva","alias":"El Ingeniero / El H","cartels":["beltran_leyva","sinaloa"],"born":1962,"died":2018,"role":"Led the Beltrán Leyva Organization after his brother Arturo's death","status_2026":"Died 18 November 2018 of a heart attack while in Mexican custody","bio":"Born 15 February 1962 in Badiraguato, Sinaloa, Héctor Beltrán Leyva was known as 'El Ingeniero' (The Engineer) and 'El H'. He worked alongside his brothers in the Sinaloa Federation before the 2008 split, handling finance and logistics rather than street violence. After Arturo was killed by marines in Cuernavaca in December 2009, Héctor took over what remained of the Beltrán Leyva Organization, but the group had already begun fragmenting into rival successors including the Cártel del Pacífico Sur, the Cártel Independiente de Acapulco and Los Rojos, whose infighting turned Guerrero and Morelos into among the most violent regions of Mexico. Héctor kept a deliberately low profile, moving through central Mexico and reportedly living for years in San Miguel de Allende, Guanajuato, under a false identity as a businessman and art dealer. Mexican Army troops arrested him without resistance at a seafood restaurant in San Miguel de Allende on 1 October 2014 and transferred him to the Altiplano maximum-security prison near Mexico City. He remained in Mexican custody facing organised crime and money laundering charges. On 18 November 2018 he suffered chest pains and died of a heart attack at a medical centre in Toluca, aged 56.","notable":["Handled finance and logistics for the Beltrán Leyva Organization","Took command after Arturo was killed in December 2009","Lived under a false identity in San Miguel de Allende as an art dealer","Arrested without resistance at a restaurant on 1 October 2014","Died of a heart attack in custody, 18 November 2018"],"state":"SIN","lat":24.7993,"lng":-107.3938,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/H%C3%A9ctor_Beltr%C3%A1n_Leyva","https://insightcrime.org/criminal-profiles/"],"verified":false},{"id":"edgar_valdez_villarreal","name":"Edgar Valdez Villarreal","alias":"La Barbie","cartels":["beltran_leyva","sinaloa"],"born":1973,"died":0,"role":"American-born enforcer who led the Beltrán Leyva Organization's hit squads","status_2026":"Imprisoned in the US, 49-year sentence imposed June 2018","bio":"Born 11 August 1973 in Laredo, Texas, Edgar Valdez Villarreal was a US citizen and high school football player whose fair hair and blue eyes earned him the nickname 'La Barbie' from a coach. After a marijuana indictment in Texas he fled to Mexico around 1998 and joined the Beltrán Leyva wing of the Sinaloa Federation, where he commanded Los Negros, a squad formed to fight Los Zetas for control of Nuevo Laredo and later Acapulco. He became one of the most visible symbols of the drug war's brutality, credited with popularising the filming of interrogations and executions and the public display of bodies. When the Beltrán Leyva Organization split from the Sinaloa Cartel in 2008, he ran its operations in Guerrero and Morelos. After Arturo Beltrán Leyva was killed in December 2009, Valdez fought Héctor Beltrán Leyva for succession, deepening the violence around Acapulco and Cuernavaca. Mexican federal police captured him near Mexico City on 30 August 2010. He was transferred to the United States on 30 September 2015, pleaded guilty in Atlanta to drug conspiracy and money laundering, and in June 2018 was sentenced to 49 years and three months in federal prison. He is serving the sentence in the US federal system.","notable":["US citizen from Laredo, Texas who became a Mexican cartel enforcer","Commanded Los Negros against Los Zetas in Nuevo Laredo and Acapulco","Helped popularise filmed executions and public body displays","Captured near Mexico City on 30 August 2010","Sentenced in Atlanta in June 2018 to 49 years in federal prison"],"state":"GRO","lat":16.8531,"lng":-99.8237,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edgar_Valdez_Villarreal","https://www.bop.gov/inmateloc/","https://insightcrime.org/criminal-profiles/"],"verified":false},{"id":"sergio_villarreal_barragan","name":"Sergio Enrique Villarreal Barragán","alias":"El Grande / El King","cartels":["beltran_leyva","sinaloa"],"born":1969,"died":0,"role":"Former federal police officer turned Beltrán Leyva lieutenant and US cooperating witness","status_2026":"In the United States; cooperated with US prosecutors, current whereabouts undisclosed","bio":"Born 21 September 1969 in Torreón, Coahuila, Sergio Villarreal Barragán stood roughly two metres tall, earning him the nickname 'El Grande'. He served in the Policía Judicial Federal before moving into trafficking, first with the Juárez Cartel organisation and then with the Beltrán Leyva brothers within the Sinaloa Federation. He ran operations across Durango, Coahuila and the Laguna region and was among the senior figures who followed the Beltrán Leyva family when it broke from the Sinaloa Cartel in 2008. On 1 June 2010 the US Treasury sanctioned him under the Foreign Narcotics Kingpin Designation Act, freezing his American assets. After Arturo Beltrán Leyva's death in December 2009 he became one of several claimants to the organisation's remnants, in rivalry with Edgar 'La Barbie' Valdez Villarreal and Héctor Beltrán Leyva. Mexican marines arrested him in Puebla on 12 September 2010, reportedly without a shot fired. He was extradited to the United States on 23 May 2012 and agreed to cooperate with federal prosecutors, providing testimony about cartel corruption of Mexican officials. His cooperation fed into later US cases, including the prosecution of former security secretary Genaro García Luna. His current status and location have not been made public.","notable":["Former federal judicial police officer who joined the Beltrán Leyva Organization","Sanctioned under the US Kingpin Act on 1 June 2010","Arrested by marines in Puebla, 12 September 2010","Extradited to the United States on 23 May 2012","Became a cooperating witness in US cartel-corruption prosecutions"],"state":"COA","lat":25.5428,"lng":-103.4068,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sergio_Villarreal_Barrag%C3%A1n","https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/"],"verified":false},{"id":"vicente_zambada_niebla","name":"Vicente Zambada Niebla","alias":"El Vicentillo","cartels":["sinaloa","mayiza"],"born":1975,"died":0,"role":"Sinaloa Cartel logistics chief and star US cooperating witness against El Chapo","status_2026":"Sentenced 2019 to 15 years with credit for time served since 2009; no longer listed in federal Bureau of Prisons custody, whereabouts undisclosed","bio":"Born 10 May 1975 in Culiacán, Sinaloa, Vicente Zambada Niebla is the son of Ismael 'El Mayo' Zambada, the Sinaloa Cartel's long-serving co-founder. Known as 'El Vicentillo', he served as his father's logistics coordinator, arranging the movement of multi-ton cocaine loads from Colombia through Central America and Mexico using aircraft, submarines, speedboats and rail, and managing payments to corrupt officials. Mexican soldiers arrested him in Mexico City on 19 March 2009, hours after a meeting with DEA agents that became the basis of his later legal defence: he claimed the US government had granted the cartel's leadership immunity in exchange for intelligence, an argument the courts rejected. He was extradited to the United States in February 2010 and held in Chicago. He signed a cooperation agreement and in November 2018 became the prosecution's central witness at the Brooklyn trial of Joaquín 'El Chapo' Guzmán, describing the cartel's structure, its bribery of Mexican officials and decades of shipments. On 30 May 2019 a federal judge sentenced him to 15 years, crediting his time in custody since 2009, and ordered forfeiture of $1.37 billion. He is widely reported to have entered witness protection.","notable":["Son of Sinaloa co-founder Ismael 'El Mayo' Zambada","Arrested in Mexico City on 19 March 2009 and extradited in February 2010","Argued unsuccessfully that the DEA had granted the cartel immunity","Star prosecution witness at El Chapo's 2018-2019 Brooklyn trial","Sentenced to 15 years with $1.37 billion forfeiture, 30 May 2019"],"state":"SIN","lat":24.7993,"lng":-107.3938,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vicente_Zambada_Niebla","https://www.bop.gov/inmateloc/","https://insightcrime.org/criminal-profiles/"],"verified":false},{"id":"nazario_moreno_gonzalez","name":"Nazario Moreno González","alias":"El Chayo / El Más Loco / San Nazario","cartels":["familia_michoacana","templarios"],"born":1970,"died":2014,"role":"Messianic founder of La Familia Michoacana and the Knights Templar","status_2026":"Killed 9 March 2014 in Tumbiscatío, Michoacán, after being wrongly declared dead in 2010","bio":"Born 8 March 1970 in the Guanajuatillo ranchería of Apatzingán, Michoacán, Nazario Moreno González spent time in the United States before returning to Michoacán and entering the drug trade. He took over La Familia Michoacana around 2004 with José de Jesús Méndez Vargas after Carlos Rosales Mendoza's arrest. Moreno fused trafficking with a quasi-religious ideology, distributing his own book of pseudo-evangelical maxims, forbidding recruits from using the methamphetamine the group produced, and casting the cartel as a defender of Michoacán families. His organisation announced itself in September 2006 by rolling five severed heads onto a nightclub floor in Uruapan, an act that helped precipitate Felipe Calderón's militarised offensive that December. On 9 December 2010 federal police claimed to have killed him after a two-day gunfight at El Alcalde in Apatzingán, but no body was recovered; the government later conceded he may only have been wounded. He used the interval to build the Knights Templar Cartel as a splinter with elaborate rituals and codes while a cult of 'San Nazario' grew around shrines bearing his image. Mexican Army and Navy forces killed him on 9 March 2014 at Tumbiscatío, Michoacán, with two gunshot wounds to the thorax.","notable":["Founded La Familia Michoacana and later the Knights Templar","Wrote a pseudo-religious code book distributed to cartel members","Announced the cartel with five severed heads in Uruapan, September 2006","Falsely declared killed by federal police in December 2010","Actually killed by the army and navy on 9 March 2014 in Tumbiscatío"],"state":"MIC","lat":19.0847,"lng":-102.3506,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nazario_Moreno_Gonz%C3%A1lez","https://insightcrime.org/criminal-profiles/"],"verified":false},{"id":"jose_de_jesus_mendez_vargas","name":"José de Jesús Méndez Vargas","alias":"El Chango","cartels":["familia_michoacana"],"born":1961,"died":0,"role":"La Familia Michoacana co-founder and last leader before the group split","status_2026":"In US custody after transfer from Mexico on 27 February 2025; facing federal charges in New York","bio":"Born 28 February 1961 in El Aguaje, Michoacán, José de Jesús Méndez Vargas was nicknamed 'El Chango' (The Monkey). He co-founded La Familia Michoacana with Nazario Moreno González and Servando 'La Tuta' Gómez, building an organisation that combined industrial-scale methamphetamine production with extortion of Michoacán's agriculture and commerce and a public rhetoric of social protection. The US Treasury sanctioned him under the Kingpin Act in February 2010, freezing his American assets. When Moreno was reported killed in December 2010, Méndez claimed leadership, but his authority was immediately contested by Gómez and Enrique Plancarte, who broke away in early 2011 to form the Knights Templar Cartel. The resulting internal war shattered La Familia and left Méndez commanding a diminished faction. Mexican federal police arrested him at a road checkpoint in the state of Aguascalientes on 21 June 2011, effectively ending the original organisation. He was held in Mexican maximum security for well over a decade; a Mexican court rejected his challenge to extradition in April 2014, but the transfer stalled for years. On 27 February 2025 he was among 29 prisoners handed to US authorities, and he faces drug trafficking, extortion, kidnapping and murder allegations in a New York federal court.","notable":["Co-founded La Familia Michoacana with Nazario Moreno and La Tuta","Sanctioned under the US Kingpin Act in February 2010","Lost control of the cartel when the Knights Templar split off in 2011","Arrested at a checkpoint in Aguascalientes, 21 June 2011","Transferred to US custody on 27 February 2025"],"state":"MIC","lat":19.0847,"lng":-102.3506,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jos%C3%A9_de_Jes%C3%BAs_M%C3%A9ndez_Vargas","https://insightcrime.org/criminal-profiles/"],"verified":false},{"id":"servando_gomez_martinez","name":"Servando Gómez Martínez","alias":"La Tuta","cartels":["familia_michoacana","templarios"],"born":1966,"died":0,"role":"Schoolteacher turned Knights Templar leader and media propagandist","status_2026":"In US custody after being sent from Mexico on 12 August 2025; previously sentenced in Mexico to 55 years in 2019","bio":"Born 6 February 1966 in Arteaga, Michoacán, Servando Gómez Martínez trained and worked as a primary schoolteacher, remaining on the Michoacán education payroll into 2010, which gave him the alias 'La Tuta' (The Teacher). He co-founded La Familia Michoacana in 2006 and served as its operational chief and public voice, giving telephone interviews to radio and television and filming videos in which he cast the cartel as a protector of Michoacán against outsiders. After Nazario Moreno's reported death in 2010, Gómez broke with José de Jesús Méndez and founded the Knights Templar Cartel with Enrique Plancarte in early 2011. Under his leadership the group extended extortion across Michoacán's lime, avocado, cattle and iron ore industries and took control of the port of Lázaro Cárdenas, provoking the 2013 rise of the autodefensa militias led by José Manuel Mireles and Hipólito Mora. Videos surfaced in 2014 showing him meeting politicians and journalists, deepening a corruption scandal. Federal police arrested him in a house in Morelia on 27 February 2015. In June 2019 a Mexican court sentenced him to 55 years for a 2011 kidnapping. On 12 August 2025 Mexico sent him to the United States, where he faces cocaine and methamphetamine trafficking charges.","notable":["Former primary schoolteacher, hence the alias 'La Tuta'","Co-founded La Familia Michoacana and then the Knights Templar","Ran extortion of Michoacán's lime, avocado and iron ore economies","Arrested in Morelia on 27 February 2015; sentenced to 55 years in 2019","Sent to US custody on 12 August 2025"],"state":"MIC","lat":18.3833,"lng":-102.2833,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Servando_G%C3%B3mez_Mart%C3%ADnez","https://insightcrime.org/criminal-profiles/","https://www.bop.gov/inmateloc/"],"verified":false},{"id":"enrique_plancarte_solis","name":"Enrique Plancarte Solís","alias":"Kike","cartels":["familia_michoacana","templarios"],"born":1970,"died":2014,"role":"Knights Templar co-founder who ran methamphetamine production","status_2026":"Killed 31 March 2014 in Colón, Querétaro","bio":"Born 14 September 1970 in Nueva Italia, Michoacán, Enrique Plancarte Solís was a senior figure in La Familia Michoacana before helping create the Knights Templar Cartel in early 2011 alongside Servando 'La Tuta' Gómez, following Nazario Moreno's reported death. Within both organisations his principal responsibility was methamphetamine: coordinating the import of precursor chemicals through the Pacific port of Lázaro Cárdenas, running clandestine laboratories in the Michoacán highlands, and organising smuggling routes into the United States. The US Treasury Department designated him under the Foreign Narcotics Kingpin Designation Act on 25 February 2010, and both Mexican and American authorities placed substantial rewards on him. He was also linked to the Knights Templar's extortion of agriculture and mining and to its control of local politics in the Tierra Caliente. As the autodefensa uprising and a federal offensive rolled back the cartel across Michoacán during 2013 and early 2014, Plancarte fled the state. Mexican Navy personnel killed him in a firefight in Colón, Querétaro, on 31 March 2014, three weeks after Nazario Moreno was killed. His daughter, the singer Melissa Plancarte, publicly acknowledged him as her father that year; several relatives were arrested in the months that followed.","notable":["Co-founded the Knights Templar Cartel with La Tuta in 2011","Coordinated methamphetamine production and precursor imports","Sanctioned under the US Kingpin Act on 25 February 2010","Killed by the Mexican Navy in Colón, Querétaro, 31 March 2014","His daughter is the Mexican singer Melissa Plancarte"],"state":"MIC","lat":19.0167,"lng":-102.1,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enrique_Plancarte_Sol%C3%ADs","https://insightcrime.org/criminal-profiles/"],"verified":false},{"id":"armando_valencia_cornelio","name":"Armando Valencia Cornelio","alias":"Maradona","cartels":["milenio"],"born":0,"died":0,"role":"Founder of the Milenio (Valencia) Cartel, the organisation CJNG grew out of","status_2026":"Reported released from Mexican prison around 2020 and said to be seriously ill; no confirmed public status","bio":"Armando Valencia Cornelio led the Valencia family organisation known as the Milenio Cartel, rooted in Aguililla and the Michoacán Tierra Caliente. The family began as agricultural producers before moving into cannabis and opium poppy cultivation in Michoacán and Jalisco in the late 1970s and 1980s. Under Valencia Cornelio the group built direct relationships with Colombian suppliers, including figures tied to the Medellín network, during the mid-1990s, and by the early 2000s had become a significant cocaine transporter and an early mover into synthetic drugs, operating largely out of Guadalajara, Jalisco. The organisation worked in alliance with the Sinaloa Federation, particularly with Ignacio 'Nacho' Coronel Villarreal, functioning in effect as a Sinaloa branch. Mexican authorities captured Valencia Cornelio on 15 August 2003, and leadership passed to Luis Valencia Valencia and Óscar Nava Valencia. After Nava Valencia's capture in 2009 the Milenio Cartel fractured; one of the surviving factions, led by Nemesio 'El Mencho' Oseguera Cervantes, became the Cártel Jalisco Nueva Generación, now among the most powerful criminal organisations in the hemisphere. Valencia Cornelio was reported to have been released early from prison by around July 2020 and to be suffering from cancer; no authoritative account of his situation has been published since.","notable":["Built the Michoacán-based Milenio or Valencia Cartel","Established direct cocaine supply links with Colombian traffickers","Operated as a branch of the Sinaloa Federation under Nacho Coronel","Captured on 15 August 2003, ending his active leadership","The cartel's 2009 collapse produced the CJNG under El Mencho"],"state":"MIC","lat":18.7333,"lng":-102.7833,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Milenio_Cartel","https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/"],"verified":false},{"id":"teodoro_garcia_simental","name":"Teodoro García Simental","alias":"El Teo / Tres Letras","cartels":["tijuana","sinaloa"],"born":1974,"died":0,"role":"Tijuana Cartel commander whose 2008 defection set off the battle for Tijuana","status_2026":"Imprisoned in Mexico since his January 2010 capture; no confirmed transfer to US custody","bio":"Born in 1974 in Culiacán, Sinaloa, Teodoro García Simental began working for the Tijuana Cartel around 1995 with his brother Marco Antonio, serving as an enforcer under Ramón Arellano Félix. As the Arellano Félix Organization weakened through successive arrests, García built an autonomous network in eastern Tijuana funded heavily by kidnapping for ransom and extortion rather than trafficking alone. In April 2008, after the capture of Eduardo Arellano Félix and a dispute with the family's new leadership under Fernando Sánchez Arellano, he defected and forged an alliance with the Sinaloa Cartel. The split turned Tijuana into one of the drug war's most violent theatres through 2008 and 2009, with bodies hung from bridges and near-daily gun battles. His organisation became notorious for the work of Santiago Meza López, 'El Pozolero' (The Stew Maker), who confessed to dissolving more than three hundred corpses in caustic soda on García's orders. The Mexican government offered a reward of about two million dollars for him. Federal police captured him on 12 January 2010 in La Paz, Baja California Sur. He has since been held in Mexican maximum-security prison on organised crime, kidnapping and homicide charges.","notable":["Defected from the Tijuana Cartel to the Sinaloa Cartel in April 2008","His split triggered the 2008-2009 battle for Tijuana","Employed Santiago Meza, 'El Pozolero', who dissolved over 300 bodies","Built a criminal economy centred on kidnapping and extortion","Captured in La Paz, Baja California Sur, 12 January 2010"],"state":"BCN","lat":32.5149,"lng":-117.0382,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teodoro_Garc%C3%ADa_Simental","https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/"],"verified":false},{"id":"zhenli_ye_gon","name":"Zhenli Ye Gon","alias":"","cartels":["other"],"born":1963,"died":0,"role":"Chinese-Mexican pharmaceutical importer at the centre of the largest cash seizure in drug-war history","status_2026":"Held in Mexico since his October 2016 extradition; no final conviction publicly confirmed","bio":"Born 31 January 1963 in Shanghai, Zhenli Ye Gon emigrated to Mexico and became a naturalised Mexican citizen in 2002. He owned Unimed Pharm Chem México, a company licensed to import pharmaceutical chemicals, and built a plant in Toluca, Estado de México. Mexican and US authorities alleged the firm was a front for importing pseudoephedrine and other methamphetamine precursors from Asia on an industrial scale, feeding the synthetic drug boom that reshaped Mexican organised crime in the 2000s. On 15 March 2007 Mexican federal agents raided his mansion in the Lomas de Chapultepec district of Mexico City and seized roughly $207 million in cash, mostly US hundred-dollar bills stacked in wardrobes, along with jewellery and weapons; it remains the largest cash seizure ever made in a drug investigation. Ye Gon fled to the United States and was arrested in Wheaton, Maryland, on 23 July 2007. He claimed the money belonged to Mexican political figures who had coerced him into storing it, an allegation never substantiated. US prosecutors dismissed all charges with prejudice on 28 August 2009. After years of litigation he was extradited to Mexico on 18 October 2016 to face organised crime and drug charges and has remained in Mexican custody since.","notable":["Seizure of about $207 million in cash at his Mexico City home, March 2007","Owner of Unimed Pharm Chem México, accused of importing meth precursors","Arrested in Maryland in July 2007; US charges dismissed in August 2009","Extradited from the United States to Mexico on 18 October 2016","Claimed the cash belonged to Mexican politicians, never substantiated"],"state":"CMX","lat":19.4326,"lng":-99.1332,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zhenli_Ye_Gon","https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zhenli_Ye_Gon"],"verified":false},{"id":"felipe_calderon","name":"Felipe Calderón Hinojosa","alias":"","cartels":[],"born":1962,"died":0,"role":"President of Mexico 2006-2012 who launched the militarised war on the cartels","status_2026":"Alive; private citizen, never charged; under sustained public pressure over García Luna's conviction","bio":"Born 18 August 1962 in Morelia, Michoacán, Felipe Calderón was a career National Action Party politician who won the 2006 presidential election by a margin of less than one percent amid disputed results. Seeking to establish authority, he made public security his defining project. Within days of taking office on 1 December 2006 he launched Operativo Conjunto Michoacán, deploying thousands of soldiers and federal police to his home state against La Familia Michoacana, and subsequently extended military operations to Baja California, Chihuahua, Tamaulipas and Guerrero. His strategy centred on capturing or killing cartel leaders, a 'kingpin' approach that removed many bosses but fragmented organisations into more numerous and often more violent successors. Homicides rose sharply through his term, with credible estimates of between sixty thousand and one hundred thousand drug-related deaths and tens of thousands of disappearances. His government also negotiated the Mérida Initiative with the United States. He left office on 30 November 2012 and took a fellowship at the Harvard Kennedy School. The February 2023 US conviction of his security secretary Genaro García Luna for taking Sinaloa Cartel bribes intensified calls in Mexico for him to be investigated; he has denied any knowledge of the corruption and has not been charged.","notable":["Launched Operativo Conjunto Michoacán in December 2006, opening the drug war","Pursued a kingpin-capture strategy that fragmented the cartels","Negotiated the Mérida Initiative security package with Washington","Tens of thousands of drug-related deaths occurred during his six-year term","Faced calls for investigation after García Luna's 2023 US conviction"],"state":"MIC","lat":19.706,"lng":-101.195,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Felipe_Calder%C3%B3n","https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/"],"verified":false},{"id":"genaro_garcia_luna","name":"Genaro García Luna","alias":"","cartels":[],"born":1968,"died":0,"role":"Calderón's security secretary, convicted in the US of taking Sinaloa Cartel bribes","status_2026":"Imprisoned in the US, 38-year sentence imposed October 2024, held at ADX Florence","bio":"Born in 1968 in Mexico City, Genaro García Luna trained as a mechanical engineer and rose through Mexico's intelligence service before being appointed founding director of the Agencia Federal de Investigación in 2001 under Vicente Fox. In December 2006 Felipe Calderón made him Secretary of Public Security, the post from which he directed the federal police component of the drug war until 2012, becoming Washington's principal Mexican counterpart and a conduit for hundreds of millions of dollars in Mérida Initiative assistance. After leaving office he moved to Florida and ran security consultancies. US prosecutors in Brooklyn indicted him in December 2019, alleging he had accepted multimillion-dollar cash bribes from the Sinaloa Cartel in exchange for protecting its shipments, tipping it off about operations and steering enforcement against its rivals. Witnesses at trial included Jesús 'El Rey' Zambada and Sergio Villarreal Barragán. On 21 February 2023 a jury convicted him on all five counts, including engaging in a continuing criminal enterprise and cocaine trafficking conspiracy. In October 2024 he was sentenced to 38 years and one month in federal prison and is held at ADX Florence, Colorado. In May 2025 a Florida court ordered him and his wife to pay about $2.4 billion in damages to the Mexican government.","notable":["Founding director of the AFI, 2001-2006, then security secretary 2006-2012","Principal US counterpart during the Mérida Initiative","Convicted in Brooklyn on all counts on 21 February 2023","Sentenced in October 2024 to 38 years, held at ADX Florence","Ordered in May 2025 to pay Mexico about $2.4 billion in damages"],"state":"CMX","lat":19.4326,"lng":-99.1332,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Genaro_Garc%C3%ADa_Luna","https://www.bop.gov/inmateloc/","https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/"],"verified":false},{"id":"vicente_fox","name":"Vicente Fox Quesada","alias":"","cartels":[],"born":1942,"died":0,"role":"President of Mexico 2000-2006 whose term preceded the militarised drug war","status_2026":"Alive; retired at his Guanajuato ranch, prominent advocate of drug legalisation","bio":"Born 2 July 1942 in Mexico City and raised on a ranch in Guanajuato, Vicente Fox was a Coca-Cola executive and Guanajuato governor before winning the July 2000 presidential election for the National Action Party, ending seventy-one years of continuous rule by the Institutional Revolutionary Party. That political rupture also disrupted the informal arrangements through which the old ruling party had regulated drug trafficking, contributing to the fragmentation and competition among cartels that followed. Fox created the Agencia Federal de Investigación under Genaro García Luna in 2001 and used the army against traffickers, particularly after Joaquín 'El Chapo' Guzmán escaped from Puente Grande prison in January 2001, weeks after Fox took office. His government captured senior figures including Benjamín Arellano Félix in 2002 and Osiel Cárdenas Guillén in 2003, and extradited record numbers of suspects to the United States. Violence nonetheless climbed sharply in Nuevo Laredo, Michoacán and Baja California during his final years, and in 2005 he deployed federal forces under Operación México Seguro, a precursor to Calderón's offensive. He left office on 30 November 2006. In retirement at Rancho San Cristóbal in Guanajuato he became an outspoken critic of prohibition, calling for the legalisation of cannabis and other drugs.","notable":["Ended 71 years of PRI rule with his 2000 election victory","El Chapo escaped from Puente Grande weeks into his term, January 2001","Oversaw the capture of Benjamín Arellano Félix and Osiel Cárdenas","Launched Operación México Seguro in 2005, a precursor to Calderón's war","Became a leading advocate of drug legalisation after leaving office"],"state":"GUA","lat":21.0189,"lng":-101.8597,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vicente_Fox","https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/"],"verified":false},{"id":"enrique_pena_nieto","name":"Enrique Peña Nieto","alias":"","cartels":[],"born":1966,"died":0,"role":"President of Mexico 2012-2018 who recaptured El Chapo twice","status_2026":"Alive; reported to be living in Spain, named in corruption investigations but not charged","bio":"Born 20 July 1966 in Atlacomulco, Estado de México, Enrique Peña Nieto governed his home state from 2005 to 2011 before returning the Institutional Revolutionary Party to the presidency on 1 December 2012. He campaigned on shifting attention away from the drug war toward economic reform, and his government rhetorically de-emphasised cartel violence while structurally continuing Calderón's approach, replacing the federal police apparatus with a new Gendarmería and relying heavily on the marines. His term produced spectacular captures, including Miguel Ángel Treviño Morales in July 2013, Joaquín 'El Chapo' Guzmán in February 2014, Héctor Beltrán Leyva in October 2014 and Servando Gómez in February 2015, but also catastrophic failures: Guzmán tunnelled out of Altiplano prison in July 2015 before being recaptured in January 2016 and extradited in January 2017. The September 2014 disappearance of 43 students from Ayotzinapa in Iguala, Guerrero, and the government's discredited 'historical truth' account of it, became the defining scandal of his presidency, alongside the 2014 Tlatlaya army killings and the Casa Blanca conflict-of-interest affair. Homicides reached record highs in his final year. Since leaving office on 30 November 2018 he has lived largely abroad, reportedly in Spain, and has been named in Odebrecht and campaign-finance investigations without being charged.","notable":["Oversaw the 2014 capture, 2015 escape and 2016 recapture of El Chapo","Extradited El Chapo to the United States in January 2017","Presided over the Ayotzinapa disappearances and the 'historical truth' scandal","Homicides reached then-record levels by the end of his term","Named in Odebrecht and campaign-finance probes; not charged"],"state":"MEX","lat":19.2826,"lng":-99.6557,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enrique_Pe%C3%B1a_Nieto","https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/"],"verified":false},{"id":"jose_manuel_mireles","name":"José Manuel Mireles Valverde","alias":"El Doctor","cartels":["autodefensas"],"born":1958,"died":2020,"role":"Physician who led the Michoacán autodefensa uprising against the Knights Templar","status_2026":"Died 25 November 2020 of COVID-19","bio":"Born 24 October 1958 in Michoacán, José Manuel Mireles Valverde practised as a physician in Tepalcatepec in the Tierra Caliente. After years of Knights Templar extortion, kidnappings and killings touching his own family, he helped launch an armed self-defence movement on 24 February 2013 in Tepalcatepec, the same day a parallel uprising began in La Ruana under Hipólito Mora. Articulate and media-savvy, Mireles became the movement's national face, giving interviews explaining that citizens had taken up arms because the state would not protect them. Through 2013 the autodefensas expanded across dozens of Michoacán municipalities, expelling the Knights Templar from towns the federal government had failed to secure and contributing directly to the cartel's collapse. He survived a plane crash in January 2014 that he believed was an attempt on his life. When the government moved in 2014 to absorb the militias into a state Rural Defence Force, Mireles refused to disarm. Federal forces detained him with dozens of followers in Lázaro Cárdenas on 27 June 2014 on firearms charges. He spent roughly three years in prison, was released on parole on 11 May 2017 and acquitted in 2018. He later took a post in the federal ISSSTE health system and died of COVID-19 on 25 November 2020.","notable":["Physician who co-launched the Michoacán autodefensas on 24 February 2013","Became the movement's most prominent national spokesman","Autodefensas helped destroy the Knights Templar across Michoacán","Jailed on weapons charges 27 June 2014, paroled May 2017, acquitted 2018","Died of COVID-19 on 25 November 2020, aged 62"],"state":"MIC","lat":19.1878,"lng":-102.8461,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jos%C3%A9_Manuel_Mireles_Valverde","https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/"],"verified":false},{"id":"hipolito_mora","name":"Hipólito Mora Chávez","alias":"","cartels":["autodefensas"],"born":1955,"died":2023,"role":"Lime grower who founded the first Michoacán autodefensa group in La Ruana","status_2026":"Assassinated 29 June 2023 in La Ruana, Michoacán","bio":"Born 26 July 1955 in Buenavista Tomatlán, Michoacán, Hipólito Mora was a lime grower in La Ruana. He had twice been imprisoned in the United States for drug offences, in 1989 and 1993, and was deported in 1995. On 24 February 2013, after Knights Templar operatives controlling the local lime packing houses rejected his son's harvest, Mora took his pistol to confront them and rallied neighbours into an armed self-defence group, the first of the Michoacán autodefensas, launched the same day as Mireles's group in Tepalcatepec. The movement spread rapidly and broke the Knights Templar's hold over the Tierra Caliente. Mora was jailed briefly in 2014 over a factional killing and released for lack of evidence. On 16 December 2014 a gun battle in La Ruana between his fighters and a rival autodefensa faction led by Luis Antonio Torres, 'El Americano', left eleven dead including his son Manuel. Mora later joined the government-sanctioned Rural Defence Force, ran unsuccessfully for local office and then for governor of Michoacán in 2021, and continued denouncing the collusion of officials with Cárteles Unidos. On 29 June 2023 gunmen intercepted his convoy in La Ruana, opened fire and set his armoured vehicle alight, killing him and three bodyguards.","notable":["Founded the first Michoacán autodefensa group in La Ruana, 24 February 2013","Lime grower who armed neighbours against Knights Templar extortion","His son Manuel was killed in a December 2014 clash between militia factions","Ran for governor of Michoacán in 2021","Assassinated with three bodyguards in La Ruana, 29 June 2023"],"state":"MIC","lat":19.1428,"lng":-102.5464,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hip%C3%B3lito_Mora","https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/"],"verified":false},{"id":"jose_luis_abarca","name":"José Luis Abarca Velázquez","alias":"","cartels":["guerreros_unidos"],"born":1961,"died":0,"role":"Mayor of Iguala accused of ordering the attack that led to the Ayotzinapa disappearances","status_2026":"Imprisoned in Mexico since November 2014; convicted of kidnapping in 2019 and held on organised crime and money laundering charges","bio":"Born in 1961, José Luis Abarca Velázquez was a jewellery and shopping-centre businessman in Iguala, Guerrero, who served as the city's municipal president from 1 October 2012 until 4 October 2014, elected on a Party of the Democratic Revolution ticket. Prosecutors alleged that he and his wife, María de los Ángeles Pineda Villa, whose brothers had been Beltrán Leyva operatives, were tied to the Guerreros Unidos organisation, which had emerged from the collapse of the Beltrán Leyva network and dominated Iguala's heroin trade and municipal police. On the night of 26-27 September 2014, municipal police and gunmen attacked buses commandeered by students from the Ayotzinapa rural teachers' college; six people were killed, dozens injured, and 43 students were seized and disappeared. Federal authorities initially accused Abarca of ordering the students detained to stop them disrupting an event featuring his wife, a version later discredited along with the government's broader 'historical truth'. The couple fled and were captured in the Iztapalapa district of Mexico City on 4 November 2014. Abarca was sent to the Altiplano maximum-security prison. He was convicted in 2019 of the 2013 kidnapping of activist Arturo Hernández Cardona and remains imprisoned on organised crime, money laundering and illicit enrichment charges; the Ayotzinapa case against him has never been fully resolved.","notable":["Mayor of Iguala, Guerrero, from October 2012 to October 2014","Accused of ties to the Guerreros Unidos through his wife's family","Iguala police attacked Ayotzinapa students on 26-27 September 2014, 43 disappeared","Captured with his wife in Mexico City on 4 November 2014","Convicted in 2019 of the kidnapping of activist Arturo Hernández Cardona"],"state":"GRO","lat":18.3447,"lng":-99.5397,"sources":["https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jos%C3%A9_Luis_Abarca_Vel%C3%A1zquez","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jos%C3%A9_Luis_Abarca_Vel%C3%A1zquez","https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/"],"verified":false},{"id":"miguel_angel_felix_gallardo","name":"Miguel Ángel Félix Gallardo","alias":"El Padrino / El Jefe de Jefes","cartels":["guadalajara","sinaloa"],"born":1946,"died":0,"role":"Founder and boss of the Guadalajara Cartel, architect of Mexico's first national trafficking federation","status_2026":"Imprisoned in Mexico since 1989 (Cefereso No. 2, Puente Grande, Jalisco); aged 80 and in poor health; a 2022 house-arrest order was repeatedly suspended and never executed","bio":"Born 8 January 1946 in Bellavista, Culiacán, Sinaloa, Félix Gallardo served in the Sinaloa state judicial police and as a bodyguard to governor Leopoldo Sánchez Celis, ties that later shielded his trafficking. After the army's Operation Condor pushed Sinaloan growers out of the sierra, he moved to Guadalajara around 1980 and, with Ernesto Fonseca Carrillo and Rafael Caro Quintero, assembled the Guadalajara Cartel — the first Mexican organization to move Colombian cocaine at industrial scale for the Medellín Cartel, brokered through Honduran partner Juan Ramón Matta-Ballesteros. Known as 'El Padrino', he bought protection from the Dirección Federal de Seguridad, whose credentials his lieutenants carried. The February 1985 kidnapping, torture and murder of DEA agent Enrique Camarena in Guadalajara shattered that arrangement; under sustained US pressure, federal commander Guillermo González Calderoni arrested him in Guadalajara on 8 April 1989. Mexican accounts hold that from prison he parcelled out the border plazas — Tijuana to his Arellano Félix relatives, Ciudad Juárez to Rafael Aguilar Guajardo and Amado Carrillo Fuentes, the Sonoran corridor to the Caro Quintero brothers, and the Sinaloa Pacific routes to Zambada, Guzmán and Palma — a division that seeded the modern cartel map. Convicted in the Camarena case and sentenced to 40 years, he has been held in maximum-security prisons ever since.","notable":["Assembled the Guadalajara Cartel around 1980, uniting Sinaloan traffickers with Colombian cocaine supply","Bought systematic protection from the DFS, Mexico's Cold War political police","Arrested 8 April 1989 in Guadalajara for the murder of DEA agent Enrique Camarena","Credited with dividing the border into 'plazas', the template for later cartel territories","Sentenced to 40 years; a September 2022 house-arrest ruling was blocked by prosecutors and monitoring failures"],"state":"JAL","lat":20.6597,"lng":-103.3496,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miguel_%C3%81ngel_F%C3%A9lix_Gallardo","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guadalajara_Cartel","https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/rafael-caro-quintero/"],"verified":false},{"id":"rafael_caro_quintero","name":"Rafael Caro Quintero","alias":"El Narco de Narcos / RCQ","cartels":["guadalajara","caborca","sinaloa"],"born":1952,"died":0,"role":"Guadalajara Cartel co-founder, marijuana magnate, and prime suspect in the murder of DEA agent Enrique Camarena","status_2026":"In US federal custody in Brooklyn after extradition on 27 February 2025; pleaded not guilty; DOJ announced 5 August 2025 it will not seek the death penalty; plea negotiations reported March 2026 with trial set for 2027","bio":"Born 24 October 1952 in La Noria, Badiraguato, Sinaloa, Caro Quintero rose from a peasant marijuana-growing family to become the Guadalajara Cartel's most flamboyant partner alongside Félix Gallardo and Ernesto Fonseca Carrillo. His signature venture was Rancho Búfalo in Chihuahua, an industrial marijuana plantation worked by thousands of labourers, which Mexican soldiers destroyed in November 1984 after DEA intelligence — widely attributed to agent Enrique Camarena — exposed it. In revenge, gunmen seized Camarena and pilot Alfredo Zavala Avelar in Guadalajara on 7 February 1985; Camarena was tortured for some 30 hours at a Caro Quintero property on Calle Lope de Vega and killed. Caro Quintero fled to Costa Rica with his teenage girlfriend Sara Cosío and was arrested there on 4 April 1985 and returned to Mexico, where he was convicted and sentenced to 40 years. A state appellate court freed him on a jurisdictional technicality on 9 August 2013; the Supreme Court quickly voided the ruling but he had vanished, rebuilding influence in Sonora through the so-called Caborca Cartel. Recaptured on 15 July 2022 in San Simón, Sinaloa — an operation whose navy helicopter crash killed 14 personnel — he was held at Altiplano until Mexico transferred him and 28 others to US custody in February 2025.","notable":["Ran Rancho Búfalo, a marijuana plantation of roughly 1,000 hectares destroyed by the army in 1984","Prime suspect in the 1985 abduction, torture and murder of DEA agent Enrique 'Kiki' Camarena","Released on a technicality in August 2013 and a fugitive for nine years on FBI and DEA most-wanted lists","Recaptured 15 July 2022; a navy Black Hawk supporting the operation crashed, killing 14","Sent to the United States on 27 February 2025 in a transfer of 29 prisoners; US prosecutors dropped the death penalty in August 2025"],"state":"JAL","lat":20.6597,"lng":-103.3496,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rafael_Caro_Quintero","https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/rafael-caro-quintero/","https://www.justice.gov/usao-edny","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enrique_Camarena_(DEA_agent)"],"verified":false},{"id":"ernesto_fonseca_carrillo","name":"Ernesto Fonseca Carrillo","alias":"Don Neto","cartels":["guadalajara"],"born":1930,"died":0,"role":"Guadalajara Cartel co-founder and senior partner; uncle and mentor of Amado Carrillo Fuentes","status_2026":"Released 9 April 2025 at age 94 after completing his 40-year Mexican sentence; living free in Estado de México and suing the Mexican state for return of assets seized in 1985","bio":"Born 1 August 1930 in Santiago de los Caballeros, Badiraguato, Sinaloa, Ernesto Fonseca Carrillo was the oldest of the Guadalajara triumvirate and the bridge between the old sierra opium trade and the cocaine era. He trafficked heroin and marijuana from the 1960s, survived Operation Condor by relocating to Guadalajara, and there partnered with Miguel Ángel Félix Gallardo and Rafael Caro Quintero around 1980 to move Colombian cocaine north. His nephews Amado and Vicente Carrillo Fuentes learned the business under him, as did much of the later Juárez organization. Fonseca was implicated in the February 1985 kidnapping and murder of DEA agent Enrique Camarena — a recorded interrogation of the agent was made at properties tied to the group — and was arrested on 7 April 1985 in Puerto Vallarta, Jalisco, days after Caro Quintero's capture in Costa Rica. Convicted of the Camarena killing and related charges, he received a 40-year sentence, serving most of it at Almoloya/Altiplano before being moved to house arrest in Atizapán, Estado de México, in November 2016 on grounds of age and illness. He completed the sentence and was formally freed on 9 April 2025, aged 94. He remains under US indictment.","notable":["Oldest of the three Guadalajara Cartel founders and a veteran of the 1960s-70s Sinaloa heroin trade","Uncle and mentor of Amado Carrillo Fuentes, later the Juárez Cartel's 'Lord of the Skies'","Arrested 7 April 1985 in Puerto Vallarta for the Camarena murder; sentenced to 40 years","Granted house arrest in November 2016 because of age and failing health","Completed his sentence and walked free on 9 April 2025 at 94; filed suit in late 2025 to recover seized assets"],"state":"JAL","lat":20.6597,"lng":-103.3496,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ernesto_Fonseca_Carrillo","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guadalajara_Cartel","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enrique_Camarena_(DEA_agent)"],"verified":false},{"id":"enrique_camarena","name":"Enrique Camarena Salazar","alias":"Kiki","cartels":[],"born":1947,"died":1985,"role":"US DEA special agent in Guadalajara whose 1985 murder transformed US-Mexico drug policy","status_2026":"Killed February 1985; commemorated annually by Red Ribbon Week; Rafael Caro Quintero awaits trial in Brooklyn for his murder","bio":"Born 26 July 1947 in Mexicali, Baja California, and raised in Calexico, California, Enrique 'Kiki' Camarena served in the US Marine Corps and worked as a police officer and investigator before joining the Drug Enforcement Administration in 1974. After postings in Calexico and Fresno he was assigned to the DEA's Guadalajara resident office in 1980, where he proved unusually effective at recruiting informants inside the Guadalajara Cartel. His intelligence underpinned the November 1984 army raid that destroyed Rancho Búfalo, a vast marijuana plantation in Chihuahua linked to Rafael Caro Quintero and valued in the billions of dollars. On 7 February 1985 gunmen — including men carrying Dirección Federal de Seguridad credentials — abducted Camarena outside the US consulate in Guadalajara; Mexican pilot and DEA source Alfredo Zavala Avelar was seized the same day. Camarena was interrogated and tortured for roughly 30 hours, the sessions recorded on tape, and died on 9 February. The bodies were found on 5 March 1985 on a ranch near La Angostura, Michoacán. Washington's response — border shutdowns, the drug-certification regime, and Operation Leyenda, the largest homicide investigation in DEA history — broke the Guadalajara Cartel and reshaped bilateral relations for a generation.","notable":["Joined the DEA in 1974 and was posted to Guadalajara in 1980","His intelligence led to the November 1984 destruction of the Rancho Búfalo marijuana plantation","Abducted 7 February 1985 outside the US consulate in Guadalajara and murdered on 9 February","Body recovered 5 March 1985 near La Angostura, Michoacán","His murder triggered Operation Leyenda, the US drug-certification law, and Red Ribbon Week"],"state":"JAL","lat":20.6597,"lng":-103.3496,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enrique_Camarena_(DEA_agent)","https://www.dea.gov/history","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guadalajara_Cartel"],"verified":false},{"id":"juan_ramon_matta_ballesteros","name":"Juan Ramón Matta-Ballesteros","alias":"El Negro","cartels":["guadalajara","other"],"born":1945,"died":2025,"role":"Honduran broker who linked the Medellín Cartel to the Guadalajara Cartel and pioneered the Central American cocaine corridor","status_2026":"Died 30 October 2025, aged 80, in a US federal prison after 37 years of incarceration","bio":"Born 12 January 1945 in Tegucigalpa, Honduras, Matta-Ballesteros became the essential middleman of the 1980s cocaine trade, connecting Pablo Escobar's Medellín Cartel with Miguel Ángel Félix Gallardo's Guadalajara organization. He built airstrips and transport networks across Honduras and owned SETCO, an airline that US congressional investigators later found had been used to fly supplies to the Nicaraguan Contras while also moving cocaine — the basis of enduring allegations about US tolerance of his operations. Arrested in the United States in 1985, he escaped from a minimum-security prison in Florida and returned to Honduras, where he was legally untouchable because the constitution barred extradition of nationals. On 5 April 1988 Honduran forces seized him and handed him to US marshals in the Dominican Republic in what critics called an illegal rendition; riots in Tegucigalpa that week burned part of the US embassy annex. Convicted in Los Angeles under Operation Leyenda for his role in the kidnapping of DEA agent Enrique Camarena, and separately on narcotics charges, he received multiple life terms and spent years at ADX Florence and other maximum-security facilities. He died in US custody on 30 October 2025 at the age of 80, still serving his sentences.","notable":["Linked the Medellín and Guadalajara cartels, opening the Central American cocaine corridor","Owned SETCO, an airline tied to both cocaine flights and Contra resupply operations","Escaped a US prison in 1986 and was seized in Honduras on 5 April 1988 in a contested rendition","Convicted in Los Angeles for the kidnapping of DEA agent Enrique Camarena","Died 30 October 2025 in a US federal prison after 37 years inside"],"state":"JAL","lat":20.6597,"lng":-103.3496,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Ram%C3%B3n_Matta-Ballesteros","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enrique_Camarena_(DEA_agent)","https://insightcrime.org/"],"verified":false},{"id":"pablo_acosta_villarreal","name":"Pablo Acosta Villarreal","alias":"El Zorro de Ojinaga","cartels":["juarez"],"born":1937,"died":1987,"role":"Boss of the Ojinaga plaza on the Chihuahua-Texas border and mentor to Amado Carrillo Fuentes","status_2026":"Killed April 1987 in a Mexican federal police raid at Santa Elena, Chihuahua","bio":"Born 26 January 1937 in Manuel Benavides, Chihuahua, Pablo Acosta Villarreal grew up between the Big Bend borderlands and West Texas, where he served time in a US prison before returning to Mexico. By the late 1970s he had made the dusty river town of Ojinaga his fiefdom, controlling smuggling along roughly 200 miles of the Chihuahua-Texas frontier with the tolerance of federal police commanders and the Dirección Federal de Seguridad. At his height between 1984 and 1986 he moved an estimated 60 tons of cocaine a year for Colombian suppliers, alongside marijuana and heroin, and Ojinaga became the first true Mexican cocaine transshipment hub. Among the young operators who learned the trade under him was Amado Carrillo Fuentes, whom Acosta took on as a protégé and who would later inherit and vastly expand the network as the Juárez Cartel. Acosta gave a remarkable series of interviews to American journalist Terrence Poppa, describing his business and his protectors — publicity that made him expendable. On 24 April 1987 Mexican federal police helicopters, with FBI logistical support, raided the tiny Rio Grande hamlet of Santa Elena, Chihuahua; Acosta died in the ensuing firefight, aged 50.","notable":["Controlled the Ojinaga plaza, the first major Mexican cocaine transshipment point on the Texas border","Moved an estimated 60 tons of cocaine a year at his peak in the mid-1980s","Mentored Amado Carrillo Fuentes, the future 'Lord of the Skies'","Gave candid interviews to reporter Terrence Poppa, later the book 'Drug Lord'","Killed 24 April 1987 in a federal police raid on Santa Elena, Chihuahua, backed by the FBI"],"state":"CHH","lat":29.5644,"lng":-104.4158,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pablo_Acosta_Villarreal","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ju%C3%A1rez_Cartel","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rafael_Aguilar_Guajardo"],"verified":false},{"id":"juan_nepomuceno_guerra","name":"Juan Nepomuceno Guerra","alias":"Don Juan","cartels":["gulf"],"born":1915,"died":2001,"role":"Matamoros smuggling patriarch who founded the organization that became the Gulf Cartel","status_2026":"Died July 2001 of respiratory illness in Matamoros, having spent almost no time in prison","bio":"Born 18 July 1915 in Matamoros, Tamaulipas, Juan Nepomuceno Guerra began smuggling whisky across the Rio Grande into south Texas during the 1930s, in the last years of the American Prohibition economy. Over the following decades he built a contraband empire in the lower Rio Grande valley — liquor, cigarettes, stolen cars, guns and eventually heroin and marijuana — sustained less by violence than by a dense web of political protection inside the ruling PRI, local law enforcement and Tamaulipas business. Operating from his Piedras Negras restaurant in Matamoros, where he held court openly for decades, he became known as the godfather of the border smuggling trade and the model for later cartel-state accommodation. In the 1970s and 1980s he brought his nephew Juan García Ábrego into the business; García Ábrego reoriented the organization toward cocaine, negotiating directly with the Cali Cartel and turning his uncle's contraband network into the modern Gulf Cartel. Guerra was briefly detained in 1991 in connection with a killing but was never seriously prosecuted, and by most accounts spent no more than a few hours in a cell across his entire career. He died in Matamoros on 12 July 2001, aged 85, of respiratory disease.","notable":["Began as a Prohibition-era whisky smuggler across the Rio Grande in the 1930s","Built the Matamoros contraband network that became the Gulf Cartel","Sustained by decades of PRI political protection rather than by violence","Brought his nephew Juan García Ábrego into the trade, who converted it to cocaine","Never meaningfully prosecuted; died of natural causes in Matamoros on 12 July 2001"],"state":"TAM","lat":25.8797,"lng":-97.5042,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Nepomuceno_Guerra","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gulf_Cartel","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Garc%C3%ADa_%C3%81brego"],"verified":false},{"id":"juan_garcia_abrego","name":"Juan García Ábrego","alias":"La Muñeca","cartels":["gulf"],"born":1944,"died":0,"role":"Gulf Cartel boss of the late 1980s and early 1990s; first trafficker placed on the FBI's Ten Most Wanted list","status_2026":"Imprisoned in the United States since 1996 serving eleven consecutive life sentences; reported held at USP Hazelton, West Virginia","bio":"Born 13 September 1944 in Matamoros, Tamaulipas, and raised partly in south Texas, Juan García Ábrego learned smuggling from his uncle Juan Nepomuceno Guerra. In the 1980s he pushed the family's contraband network into cocaine, striking deals directly with Colombia's Cali Cartel in which the Gulf organization took a share of each shipment rather than a flat transport fee — a shift that made Mexican traffickers principals rather than couriers and multiplied their wealth. By the early 1990s he controlled the eastern corridor into Texas and had bought protection reaching into the federal judicial police and, US prosecutors alleged, the highest levels of the Salinas administration; his cartel was estimated to move a third of the cocaine entering the United States. In 1995 he became the first drug trafficker added to the FBI's Ten Most Wanted Fugitives list. Mexican federal agents captured him on a ranch outside Monterrey, Nuevo León, on 14 January 1996 and, because he was deemed a US citizen by birth, he was flown to Houston within hours rather than going through extradition. A Houston jury convicted him in October 1996 on 22 counts of drug trafficking, money laundering and running a continuing criminal enterprise; Judge Ewing Werlein imposed eleven consecutive life terms.","notable":["Restructured Gulf Cartel deals with the Cali Cartel to take payment in cocaine, not cash","At his peak reputedly moved about a third of US-bound cocaine","First drug trafficker named to the FBI's Ten Most Wanted Fugitives list, in 1995","Captured near Monterrey on 14 January 1996 and flown to Houston the same day as a US citizen","Convicted on 22 counts and sentenced to eleven consecutive life terms; still incarcerated in 2026"],"state":"TAM","lat":25.8797,"lng":-97.5042,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Garc%C3%ADa_%C3%81brego","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gulf_Cartel","https://www.justice.gov/"],"verified":false},{"id":"rafael_aguilar_guajardo","name":"Rafael Aguilar Guajardo","alias":"El Comandante","cartels":["juarez","guadalajara"],"born":1950,"died":1993,"role":"DFS commander turned co-founder and first boss of the Juárez Cartel","status_2026":"Assassinated April 1993 in Cancún, Quintana Roo, on Amado Carrillo Fuentes's orders","bio":"Born in 1950, Rafael Aguilar Guajardo was a commander in the Dirección Federal de Seguridad, the Mexican interior ministry's political police, and embodied the fusion of state security apparatus and drug trafficking that defined the 1980s. Using DFS credentials and protection, he operated inside the Guadalajara Cartel's orbit while building his own interests along the Chihuahua border. When Pablo Acosta Villarreal was killed in April 1987 and the DFS was being dismantled in the fallout from the Camarena murder, Aguilar Guajardo took over the Ciudad Juárez corridor, becoming the first recognised head of what would be called the Juárez Cartel. He promoted the young Amado Carrillo Fuentes, a nephew of Ernesto Fonseca Carrillo, to be his second-in-command and operational chief, an arrangement that lasted until Carrillo Fuentes decided he no longer needed a patron. On 12 April 1993, while on holiday with his family in Cancún, Quintana Roo, Aguilar Guajardo was shot dead outside a restaurant on the resort strip as he returned from a tourist excursion; family members and a bystander were also hit. Reporting attributes the killing to Carrillo Fuentes, allegedly ordered days after Aguilar threatened to expose his high-level government contacts. Carrillo Fuentes took sole control of the Juárez organization.","notable":["Served as a commander of the Dirección Federal de Seguridad while trafficking drugs","Took over the Ciudad Juárez plaza after Pablo Acosta's death in April 1987","First recognised leader of the Juárez Cartel, with Amado Carrillo Fuentes as his deputy","Shot dead in Cancún on 12 April 1993 while vacationing with his family","His killing, attributed to Carrillo Fuentes, handed the Juárez Cartel to the 'Lord of the Skies'"],"state":"CHH","lat":31.6904,"lng":-106.4245,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rafael_Aguilar_Guajardo","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ju%C3%A1rez_Cartel","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amado_Carrillo_Fuentes"],"verified":false},{"id":"amado_carrillo_fuentes","name":"Amado Carrillo Fuentes","alias":"El Señor de los Cielos","cartels":["juarez","guadalajara"],"born":1954,"died":1997,"role":"Juárez Cartel boss who industrialised cocaine transport by air and became the richest trafficker of his era","status_2026":"Died July 1997 in Mexico City after cosmetic surgery intended to disguise his appearance","bio":"Born 17 December 1954 in Guamuchilito, Navolato, Sinaloa, Amado Carrillo Fuentes entered the trade under his uncle Ernesto Fonseca Carrillo in the Guadalajara Cartel and learned border operations in Ojinaga from Pablo Acosta Villarreal and Rafael Aguilar Guajardo. After Aguilar Guajardo's assassination in Cancún in April 1993 — which reporting attributes to Carrillo Fuentes — he took undisputed control of the Juárez Cartel. His innovation was scale and altitude: he assembled a fleet of commercial-sized jets, including Boeing 727s, to fly multi-ton cocaine loads from Colombia into northern Mexico, earning the name 'Lord of the Skies' and, by US estimates, a fortune approaching 25 billion dollars. He also perfected the purchase of the state. In February 1997 Mexico's newly appointed anti-drug czar, General Jesús Gutiérrez Rebollo, was arrested for taking his money, a scandal that broke days before a US certification vote. With pressure mounting, Carrillo Fuentes sought to change his face and fingerprints, undergoing extensive plastic surgery and liposuction at the Santa Mónica hospital in Mexico City. He died there on 4 July 1997, aged 42, of complications reported variously as anaesthetic reaction or respirator failure; two of his surgeons were later found tortured and encased in concrete.","notable":["Ran a fleet of jetliners, including Boeing 727s, to fly multi-ton cocaine loads from Colombia","Took over the Juárez Cartel after the 1993 killing of Rafael Aguilar Guajardo","Corrupted General Jesús Gutiérrez Rebollo, Mexico's anti-drug czar, exposed in February 1997","Estimated by US authorities to have amassed a fortune of some 25 billion dollars","Died 4 July 1997 in Mexico City during plastic surgery meant to change his appearance"],"state":"CHH","lat":31.6904,"lng":-106.4245,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amado_Carrillo_Fuentes","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ju%C3%A1rez_Cartel","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jes%C3%BAs_Guti%C3%A9rrez_Rebollo"],"verified":false},{"id":"vicente_carrillo_fuentes","name":"Vicente Carrillo Fuentes","alias":"El Viceroy","cartels":["juarez"],"born":1962,"died":0,"role":"Juárez Cartel boss after his brother Amado's death; commander in the Juárez-Sinaloa war of 2008-2012","status_2026":"In US federal custody after extradition on 27 February 2025; plea negotiations under way, with a hearing scheduled for October 2026","bio":"Born 16 October 1962 in Guamuchilito, Navolato, Sinaloa, Vicente Carrillo Fuentes inherited the Juárez Cartel after his brother Amado died on the operating table in July 1997. Less charismatic and more brutal than his brother, he held the Ciudad Juárez plaza through the late 1990s and 2000s with the enforcement wing La Línea, staffed heavily by serving and former Chihuahua police. His alliance with Joaquín Guzmán collapsed after the 2004 killing of his brother Rodolfo Carrillo Fuentes and his wife outside a Culiacán cinema, blamed on Guzmán's people, and the retaliatory murder of Guzmán's brother Arturo in prison. The rupture became the Juárez-Sinaloa war that from 2008 turned Ciudad Juárez into the most violent city in the world, with more than 3,000 homicides in 2010 alone and atrocities including the January 2010 Villas de Salvárcar massacre of teenagers. The Sinaloa Cartel gradually won the corridor, and the Juárez organization fragmented. Mexican federal police captured Carrillo Fuentes on 9 October 2014 in Torreón, Coahuila. A Mexican court sentenced him to 28 years in 2021, and on 27 February 2025 he was among 29 prisoners Mexico handed to the United States; he pleaded not guilty in New York and has since sought a plea agreement.","notable":["Took over the Juárez Cartel in 1997 after the death of his brother Amado","Commanded La Línea, the cartel's police-staffed enforcement wing","Fought the 2008-2012 war with the Sinaloa Cartel that made Ciudad Juárez the world's deadliest city","Captured 9 October 2014 in Torreón, Coahuila; sentenced in Mexico to 28 years in 2021","Transferred to the United States on 27 February 2025 alongside Rafael Caro Quintero"],"state":"CHH","lat":31.6904,"lng":-106.4245,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vicente_Carrillo_Fuentes","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ju%C3%A1rez_Cartel","https://www.justice.gov/usao-edny"],"verified":false},{"id":"benjamin_arellano_felix","name":"Benjamín Arellano Félix","alias":"El Min","cartels":["tijuana"],"born":1952,"died":0,"role":"Strategic head of the Tijuana Cartel (Arellano Félix Organization)","status_2026":"Imprisoned in the United States serving a 25-year sentence; reported held at USP Lee, Virginia; compassionate release denied January 2023","bio":"Born 12 March 1952 in Culiacán, Sinaloa, Benjamín Arellano Félix was the strategist of the eleven-sibling clan that inherited the Tijuana plaza after Miguel Ángel Félix Gallardo's 1989 arrest. Where his brother Ramón supplied violence, Benjamín supplied organisation: he recruited the sons of Tijuana's business elite as gunmen — the narcojuniors — bought Baja California's police and judiciary, and built a taxation system over every load crossing at San Ysidro and Otay Mesa. His war with Joaquín Guzmán defined the 1990s. On 24 May 1993 a shootout at Guadalajara airport involving Tijuana gunmen left Cardinal Juan Jesús Posadas Ocampo dead, a scandal that forced Guzmán's flight and arrest and made the Arellano Félix name notorious. The cartel also ordered the November 1997 attack that wounded Tijuana journalist Jesús Blancornelas and killed his bodyguard. Ramón was killed in Mazatlán in February 2002, and Benjamín was captured weeks later, on 9 March 2002, in Puebla. Mexico held him until his extradition to the United States in April 2011; he pleaded guilty in San Diego to racketeering and money-laundering conspiracy and was sentenced in April 2012 to 25 years. A federal judge rejected his compassionate-release petition in January 2023, calling him ruthless and inhumane.","notable":["Ran the Tijuana Cartel's strategy and corruption networks from the late 1980s","Recruited the 'narcojuniors', gunmen drawn from wealthy Tijuana families","Cartel gunmen killed Cardinal Posadas Ocampo at Guadalajara airport on 24 May 1993","Ordered the 1997 attack that wounded editor Jesús Blancornelas of the weekly Zeta","Captured 9 March 2002 in Puebla; extradited 2011 and sentenced in 2012 to 25 years in the US"],"state":"BCN","lat":32.5149,"lng":-117.0382,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benjam%C3%ADn_Arellano_F%C3%A9lix","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tijuana_Cartel","https://www.justice.gov/"],"verified":false},{"id":"ramon_arellano_felix","name":"Ramón Arellano Félix","alias":"El Mon","cartels":["tijuana"],"born":1964,"died":2002,"role":"Chief enforcer of the Tijuana Cartel and its most feared killer","status_2026":"Killed February 2002 in a shootout with a municipal police officer in Mazatlán, Sinaloa","bio":"Born 31 August 1964 in Culiacán, Sinaloa, Ramón Arellano Félix was the violence at the centre of the Tijuana Cartel his brother Benjamín organised. Through the 1990s he personally led the organization's sicarios, cultivating both the narcojuniors of Tijuana's upper class and the San Diego street gang Logan Heights, whose members he used as cross-border gunmen. He drove the cartel's war against Joaquín Guzmán, including a 1992 machine-gun attack on Guzmán's entourage at the Christine discotheque in Puerto Vallarta. On 24 May 1993 Tijuana gunmen under his command opened fire at Guadalajara's airport; Cardinal Juan Jesús Posadas Ocampo was killed in his car with fourteen shots, and the ensuing scandal made Ramón one of Mexico's most wanted men. He was indicted in the Southern District of California for conspiracy to import cocaine and marijuana, and on 18 September 1997 became the 451st fugitive added to the FBI's Ten Most Wanted list, with a two-million-dollar reward. On 10 February 2002 he was in Mazatlán, Sinaloa, reportedly hunting Ismael Zambada, when a municipal police officer, Ángel Antonio Arias Torres, stopped him; both men fired and both died. Ramón was 37.","notable":["Led the Tijuana Cartel's enforcement wing and its war against Joaquín Guzmán","Used San Diego's Logan Heights gang as cross-border assassins","Present at the 24 May 1993 Guadalajara airport shooting that killed Cardinal Posadas Ocampo","Added to the FBI's Ten Most Wanted Fugitives list on 18 September 1997","Killed 10 February 2002 in Mazatlán in an exchange of fire with a municipal police officer"],"state":"BCN","lat":32.5149,"lng":-117.0382,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ram%C3%B3n_Arellano_F%C3%A9lix","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tijuana_Cartel","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Jes%C3%BAs_Posadas_Ocampo"],"verified":false},{"id":"enedina_arellano_felix","name":"Enedina Arellano Félix","alias":"La Jefa / La Narcomamá","cartels":["tijuana"],"born":1961,"died":0,"role":"Accountant and financial chief of the Tijuana Cartel who became its leader, one of few women to head a major cartel","status_2026":"At large; long designated a kingpin by the US Treasury and named in US indictments; whereabouts undisclosed","bio":"Born 12 April 1961 in Mazatlán, Sinaloa, Enedina Arellano Félix trained as an accountant and entered the family business through its books rather than its guns. Through the 1990s she managed the Tijuana Cartel's finances, and after the departure of its money manager in 2000 she took direct charge of laundering, moving proceeds through Baja California real estate, currency exchanges and front companies. The US Treasury designated her under the Foreign Narcotics Kingpin Designation Act in June 2000. The collapse of the male leadership pushed her to the front: Ramón was killed in February 2002, Benjamín captured in March 2002, Francisco Javier 'El Tigrillo' captured at sea in August 2006, and Eduardo 'El Doctor' arrested in October 2008. From then she led the organization jointly with her son Luis Fernando Sánchez Arellano, 'El Ingeniero', steering it away from spectacular violence toward a quieter, tax-and-service model in which the surviving Tijuana network charged fees to Sinaloa Cartel traffickers using the plaza. Her son's arrest in June 2014 left her the family's senior surviving figure. Unlike her brothers she has never been captured, and Mexican and US reporting continues to describe her as a fugitive guiding what remains of the Tijuana organization.","notable":["Trained accountant who ran the Tijuana Cartel's money laundering from the 1990s","Designated by the US Treasury under the Kingpin Act in June 2000","Took over the cartel after her brothers were killed or captured between 2002 and 2008","Led jointly with her son Luis Fernando Sánchez Arellano until his June 2014 arrest","Shifted the organization to charging fees to rival traffickers rather than fighting for routes"],"state":"BCN","lat":32.5149,"lng":-117.0382,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enedina_Arellano_F%C3%A9lix","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tijuana_Cartel","https://home.treasury.gov/policy-issues/financial-sanctions/sanctions-programs-and-country-information/counter-narcotics-trafficking-sanctions"],"verified":false},{"id":"hector_palma_salazar","name":"Héctor Luis Palma Salazar","alias":"El Güero Palma","cartels":["sinaloa","guadalajara"],"born":1960,"died":0,"role":"Co-founder of the Sinaloa Cartel and partner of Joaquín Guzmán in the 1990s war with the Tijuana Cartel","status_2026":"Imprisoned in Mexico at the Altiplano maximum-security prison; acquitted of organized-crime charges in 2023 but still held; a judge ordered emergency medical care for him in April 2026","bio":"Born in 1960 in Mocorito, Sinaloa, Héctor Luis Palma Salazar began as a car thief and gunman before rising under Miguel Ángel Félix Gallardo. After the 1989 arrest of 'El Padrino' he took the Sinaloa Pacific routes in partnership with Joaquín Guzmán, Ismael Zambada and Juan José Esparragoza, forming the nucleus of the Sinaloa Cartel. His life turned on an act of revenge in 1989, when a Venezuelan associate working for the Arellano Félix brothers, Rafael Enrique Clavel, seduced Palma's wife, decapitated her and killed their two children, sending the head to Palma — an atrocity that helped ignite the Sinaloa-Tijuana war of the 1990s. Palma survived a June 1993 assassination attempt and continued running loads until 23 June 1995, when his private jet crashed near Tepic, Nayarit, and federal police found him being sheltered by a group of state police commanders; 33 officers were arrested with him. Convicted in Mexico, he was extradited to the United States in 2007, served a sentence for cocaine trafficking, and was returned to Mexico in June 2016, where he was immediately re-arrested on outstanding charges. A tribunal absolved him of organized-crime counts in 2023, but prosecutors have blocked his release.","notable":["Co-founded the Sinaloa Cartel with Guzmán, Zambada and Esparragoza after 1989","His wife and two children were murdered in 1989 by a rival working for the Arellano Félix brothers","Captured 23 June 1995 near Tepic after a plane crash, along with 33 protecting police officers","Extradited to the US in 2007, returned to Mexico in June 2016 and immediately re-arrested","Acquitted of organized-crime charges in 2023 yet still held at Altiplano; gravely ill in April 2026"],"state":"SIN","lat":24.7994,"lng":-107.3897,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/H%C3%A9ctor_Luis_Palma_Salazar","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sinaloa_Cartel","https://www.bbc.com/mundo"],"verified":false},{"id":"joaquin_guzman_loera","name":"Joaquín Guzmán Loera","alias":"El Chapo","cartels":["sinaloa","chapitos","guadalajara"],"born":1957,"died":0,"role":"Sinaloa Cartel co-founder and its most famous boss; twice escaped Mexican maximum-security prisons","status_2026":"Imprisoned in the United States for life plus 30 years at ADX Florence, Colorado; a judge denied his request for transfer to Mexico in May 2026","bio":"Born 4 April 1957 in La Tuna, Badiraguato, Sinaloa, Joaquín Guzmán Loera worked opium fields as a boy and entered trafficking under Miguel Ángel Félix Gallardo, running logistics in the 1980s Guadalajara Cartel. After the 1989 arrest of 'El Padrino' he took the Sinaloa routes with Héctor Palma, Ismael Zambada and Juan José Esparragoza, pioneering cross-border tunnels beneath Agua Prieta and Douglas and later refrigerated-truck and rail concealment. His war with the Arellano Félix brothers produced the 24 May 1993 Guadalajara airport shooting that killed Cardinal Posadas Ocampo; Guzmán fled to Guatemala and was arrested there on 9 June 1993. He escaped from Puente Grande prison in January 2001, reportedly in a laundry cart, and spent thirteen years as the world's most wanted trafficker while the Sinaloa Cartel expanded into Ciudad Juárez, Tijuana and beyond. Recaptured in Mazatlán in February 2014, he escaped again in July 2015 through a mile-long tunnel dug into his cell at Altiplano, was retaken in Los Mochis in January 2016 and extradited to the United States on 19 January 2017. A Brooklyn jury convicted him on all counts on 12 February 2019; he was sentenced in July 2019 to life plus 30 years and a 12.6 billion dollar forfeiture.","notable":["Pioneered cross-border smuggling tunnels beneath the Arizona-Sonora line in the 1990s","Escaped Puente Grande prison in January 2001 and Altiplano in July 2015 through a mile-long tunnel","Arrested in Guatemala in June 1993 after the Posadas Ocampo killing","Extradited to the US on 19 January 2017 and convicted in Brooklyn on 12 February 2019","Serving life plus 30 years at ADX Florence as inmate 89914053; 2026 transfer request denied"],"state":"SIN","lat":24.7994,"lng":-107.3897,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joaqu%C3%ADn_%22El_Chapo%22_Guzm%C3%A1n","https://www.justice.gov/usao-edny","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sinaloa_Cartel"],"verified":false},{"id":"ismael_zambada_garcia","name":"Ismael Zambada García","alias":"El Mayo","cartels":["sinaloa","mayiza"],"born":1950,"died":0,"role":"Sinaloa Cartel co-founder and its longest-surviving leader, never imprisoned in Mexico across five decades","status_2026":"Sentenced 20 July 2026 in Brooklyn to life without parole and a $15 billion forfeiture; in US federal custody after asking to serve his term in a prison medical facility rather than a supermax","bio":"Born 31 January 1950 in El Álamo, Culiacán, Sinaloa, Ismael Zambada García began as a farmhand and gunman and rose through the Guadalajara Cartel to become, after 1989, the quiet counterweight to Joaquín Guzmán within the Sinaloa organization. Where Guzmán courted celebrity, Zambada courted officials: US prosecutors alleged at his sentencing that he sustained four decades of trafficking by paying police, soldiers and politicians at every level. He built businesses in dairy, agriculture and transport around Culiacán, ran cocaine through Central America and the Pacific, and outlasted every rival, never once being arrested in Mexico. That ended on 25 July 2024, when he was flown to a private airstrip near El Paso, Texas, and taken into US custody. Zambada says he was ambushed, bound and abducted by Joaquín Guzmán López, a son of El Chapo, under the pretext of a property meeting — an act that split the Sinaloa Cartel into the warring Chapitos and Mayiza factions and plunged Sinaloa into the bloodiest fighting in its history. Prosecutors announced on 5 August 2025 they would not seek the death penalty; he pleaded guilty on 25 August 2025 to racketeering conspiracy and running a continuing criminal enterprise, and was sentenced on 20 July 2026, telling the court he renounced violence.","notable":["Co-founded the Sinaloa Cartel after 1989 and led it for 35 years without ever being arrested in Mexico","Prosecutors alleged systematic bribery of police, military and political officials","Taken to US custody on 25 July 2024 near El Paso; says he was abducted by Joaquín Guzmán López","His capture split the cartel into Chapitos and Mayiza factions, igniting the Sinaloa war of 2024-2026","Pleaded guilty 25 August 2025 and was sentenced 20 July 2026 to life plus $15 billion forfeiture"],"state":"SIN","lat":24.7994,"lng":-107.3897,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ismael_Zambada_Garc%C3%ADa","https://www.justice.gov/usao-edny","https://www.dea.gov/press-releases","https://insightcrime.org/"],"verified":false},{"id":"juan_jose_esparragoza_moreno","name":"Juan José Esparragoza Moreno","alias":"El Azul","cartels":["sinaloa","guadalajara","juarez"],"born":1949,"died":0,"role":"Sinaloa Cartel co-founder and the trade's chief mediator, a former federal security agent","status_2026":"Unresolved: reported dead in June 2014 but no body was ever produced or identified, and US authorities have continued to treat him as possibly alive","bio":"Born 3 February 1949 in Huixiopa, Badiraguato, Sinaloa, Juan José Esparragoza Moreno served as an agent of the Dirección Federal de Seguridad before moving fully into trafficking, a background that gave him unusual access to the Mexican state. Nicknamed 'El Azul' for his dark complexion, he was a member of the Guadalajara Cartel under Félix Gallardo and was imprisoned in the 1980s. After his release he became the least visible and most valuable figure of the Sinaloa Cartel's founding generation: a negotiator rather than a warlord, trusted enough to broker truces between the Sinaloa, Juárez and Beltrán Leyva organizations and to keep lines open to the Arellano Félix brothers. He was reportedly the man who arranged accommodations after major arrests and killings, and both Mexican and US officials described him as the diplomat who held the federation together. The DEA offered a five-million-dollar reward for him. In June 2014 Mexican media reported that he had died of a heart attack after a car accident near Puente Grande, Jalisco, and had been cremated; no body was recovered, no forensic confirmation was made public, and reporting through 2025 and 2026 has repeatedly revisited whether the death was staged. He remains one of Mexico's great unresolved narco disappearances.","notable":["Former Dirección Federal de Seguridad agent who became a founding Sinaloa Cartel leader","Known as the trade's mediator, brokering truces between Sinaloa, Juárez and the Beltrán Leyva","Carried a five-million-dollar US reward and was among the most wanted traffickers of the 2000s","Reported dead in June 2014 near Puente Grande, Jalisco, with no body or forensic confirmation","Repeatedly reported since as possibly alive; his fate remains officially unresolved in 2026"],"state":"SIN","lat":24.7994,"lng":-107.3897,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Jos%C3%A9_Esparragoza_Moreno","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sinaloa_Cartel","https://www.bbc.com/mundo"],"verified":false},{"id":"ignacio_coronel_villarreal","name":"Ignacio Coronel Villarreal","alias":"Nacho Coronel / El Rey del Cristal","cartels":["sinaloa"],"born":1954,"died":2010,"role":"Sinaloa Cartel leader in Jalisco who industrialised methamphetamine production","status_2026":"Killed July 2010 in a shootout with the Mexican Army in Zapopan, Jalisco","bio":"Born 1 February 1954 in Canelas, Durango, Ignacio Coronel Villarreal became one of the senior partners of the Sinaloa Cartel and its master of synthetic drugs. Operating from Guadalajara and Zapopan, Jalisco, he built the precursor-import and superlab network that turned Mexican crystal methamphetamine into a mass export product, taking over much of the business pioneered by the Amezcua Contreras brothers of Colima and earning the name 'King of Crystal'. He controlled Jalisco, Colima and Nayarit for the federation, oversaw cocaine reception on the Pacific coast, and sat alongside Guzmán, Zambada and Esparragoza in the cartel's leadership. The US Treasury designated him under the Kingpin Act and he was indicted in the United States. On 29 July 2010 Mexican Army special forces raided a house in the Colinas de San Javier neighbourhood of Zapopan; Coronel opened fire, wounding a soldier, and was shot dead. His death did not end his network. His lieutenants in Jalisco, calling themselves Los Torcidos and then the Mata Zetas, regrouped under Nemesio Oseguera Cervantes and Erick Valencia Salazar and by 2011 had constituted the Cártel Jalisco Nueva Generación, which would become the Sinaloa Cartel's principal national rival.","notable":["Known as 'El Rey del Cristal' for industrialising Mexican methamphetamine production","Controlled Jalisco, Colima and Nayarit for the Sinaloa Cartel","Sat in the federation's top leadership with Guzmán, Zambada and Esparragoza","Killed 29 July 2010 in an army raid in Zapopan, Jalisco","His Jalisco remnants regrouped in 2011 as the Cártel Jalisco Nueva Generación"],"state":"JAL","lat":20.7214,"lng":-103.3918,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ignacio_Coronel_Villarreal","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jalisco_New_Generation_Cartel","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sinaloa_Cartel"],"verified":false},{"id":"miguel_caro_quintero","name":"Miguel Ángel Caro Quintero","alias":"El Doctor","cartels":["sonora","guadalajara"],"born":1963,"died":0,"role":"Head of the Sonora Cartel, running the Caro Quintero family's marijuana and cocaine trade after his brother Rafael's imprisonment","status_2026":"Free in Mexico; completed a 17-year US sentence and was released and deported in July 2019","bio":"Born in 1963 in La Noria, Badiraguato, Sinaloa, and raised around Caborca, Sonora, Miguel Ángel Caro Quintero took over the family's trafficking interests after his elder brother Rafael was arrested in Costa Rica in April 1985. From Agua Prieta and Caborca he ran the Sonora Cartel through the late 1980s and 1990s, moving marijuana and Colombian cocaine into Arizona through desert corridors and using family members, including his sister Sandra, to launder proceeds through ranches, cattle and legitimate businesses. Indicted in Colorado and Arizona and named a Kingpin Act target, he was one of the most-wanted traffickers of the decade. Mexican federal agents captured him on 19 December 2001 in Los Mochis, Sinaloa. After years in Mexican custody he was extradited to the United States, where he pleaded guilty and received a 17-year sentence; he cut time through education and rehabilitation programmes and was released and deported to Mexico in July 2019. He has kept a low profile since, and Mexican reporting places him back in Sonora. His brother Rafael's later Caborca Cartel operated in the same territory, but Miguel has not been publicly linked to its leadership.","notable":["Led the Sonora Cartel after his brother Rafael's 1985 arrest, smuggling into Arizona","Used family members, including his sister Sandra, for laundering through ranches and cattle","Captured 19 December 2001 in Los Mochis, Sinaloa","Extradited to the US, pleaded guilty and served a 17-year sentence","Released and deported to Mexico in July 2019; free and living privately in 2026"],"state":"SON","lat":30.7167,"lng":-112.1594,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miguel_Caro_Quintero","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sonora_Cartel","https://www.justice.gov/"],"verified":false},{"id":"amezcua_contreras_brothers","name":"Amezcua Contreras brothers (José de Jesús, Adán and Luis Ignacio)","alias":"Los Reyes de las Metanfetaminas","cartels":["colima"],"born":0,"died":0,"role":"Founders of the Colima Cartel, the first organization to industrialise Mexican methamphetamine production for export","status_2026":"Organization defunct; the brothers served Mexican sentences and US extradition was blocked in 2002; their individual whereabouts are not publicly documented","bio":"Born in Colima in the late 1950s and early 1960s to a modest family, José de Jesús, Adán and Luis Ignacio Amezcua Contreras founded the Colima Cartel in 1988 and operated principally from Guadalajara. Their innovation was structural: rather than transporting Colombian cocaine for a fee, they controlled a drug from raw material to street, importing ephedrine and pseudoephedrine in bulk from suppliers in Thailand, India and the Czech Republic and cooking methamphetamine in Mexican laboratories for sale in the western United States. By the mid-1990s the DEA described them as the world's largest methamphetamine traffickers and dubbed them the Kings of Meth, a model that every later Mexican organization, from Nacho Coronel's Jalisco network to the CJNG, would copy and scale. Adán was arrested on 10 November 1997 in Colima on weapons charges and again in May 2001 for money laundering. José de Jesús and Luis Ignacio were seized in Guadalajara on 1 June 1998; several charges collapsed within days, but José de Jesús was convicted and sent to the Almoloya maximum-security prison. In May 2002 a Mexican federal court blocked his extradition to the United States, holding that the request did not satisfy Mexico's bar on extraditing people facing life imprisonment. The organization did not survive their imprisonment.","notable":["Founded the Colima Cartel in 1988, the first Mexican meth-manufacturing organization","Imported ephedrine in bulk from Thailand, India and Europe rather than buying from Colombians","Called the 'Kings of Meth' by the DEA and reckoned the world's largest meth traffickers in the 1990s","Adán arrested November 1997; José de Jesús and Luis Ignacio arrested 1 June 1998 in Guadalajara","A Mexican court blocked José de Jesús's extradition to the US in May 2002 over the life-sentence bar"],"state":"COL","lat":19.2433,"lng":-103.7247,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colima_Cartel","https://www.dea.gov/press-releases","https://insightcrime.org/"],"verified":false},{"id":"pedro_diaz_parada","name":"Pedro Díaz Parada","alias":"El Cacique de Oaxaca","cartels":["oaxaca"],"born":0,"died":0,"role":"Founder and boss of the Oaxaca (Isthmus) cartel, a marijuana empire in the Isthmus of Tehuantepec","status_2026":"Released from the Altiplano prison in October 2024; alive and living in Mexico","bio":"Born in Santa María Zoquitlán, Oaxaca, in the 1940s, Pedro Díaz Parada built the country's most durable regional marijuana organization in the Isthmus of Tehuantepec, running plantations across the Oaxacan sierra and moving product north with his brothers through a family structure that dominated the state's Pacific corridor for three decades. He also served as a corridor host for Colombian cocaine transiting from Central America. His notoriety rests on his escapes and on his defiance of the courts. First captured in 1985 and sentenced to 33 years, he reportedly told the judge who convicted him that he would go free and the judge would die; he escaped, and the judge was later murdered. He escaped again in 1990 and, after a 1992 recapture, once more, remaining at liberty for years while his organization grew. Mexican forces finally arrested him on 17 January 2007 in the Oaxacan sierra, and he was sent to the Altiplano maximum-security prison. In September 2019 reports circulated that he would serve the remainder of his term at home for health reasons, and he was released from Altiplano in October 2024, closing one of the longest continuous careers in Mexican trafficking.","notable":["Built the dominant marijuana organization of the Isthmus of Tehuantepec in Oaxaca","Escaped Mexican custody at least twice, in 1985 and 1990","Reportedly threatened the judge who sentenced him to 33 years; the judge was later killed","Recaptured 17 January 2007 in Oaxaca and imprisoned at Altiplano","Released from Altiplano in October 2024 after nearly two decades inside"],"state":"OAX","lat":16.5686,"lng":-96.1633,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pedro_D%C3%ADaz_Parada","https://insightcrime.org/","https://www.milenio.com/"],"verified":false},{"id":"jesus_gutierrez_rebollo","name":"Jesús Gutiérrez Rebollo","alias":"","cartels":[],"born":1934,"died":2013,"role":"Mexican Army general and national anti-drug czar exposed in 1997 as an employee of Amado Carrillo Fuentes","status_2026":"Died December 2013 of brain cancer at the Central Military Hospital in Mexico City while serving his sentence","bio":"Born 19 April 1934 in Jonacatepec, Morelos, Jesús Gutiérrez Rebollo rose through the Mexican Army to command the Fifth Military Region in Guadalajara, where he earned a reputation as an aggressive drug fighter by arresting figures tied to the Sinaloa and Tijuana organizations. In December 1996 President Ernesto Zedillo named him head of the Instituto Nacional para el Combate a las Drogas, making him Mexico's top counter-narcotics official; US drug czar Barry McCaffrey publicly praised him as a man of impeccable integrity. Within weeks investigators noticed he had moved into a luxury apartment in Mexico City whose rent no public salary could cover. The property belonged to the Juárez Cartel, and recordings captured him discussing payments with Amado Carrillo Fuentes in exchange for ignoring the organization's operations and targeting its rivals. He was arrested on 18 February 1997, days before the annual US drug-certification vote, in the single most damaging corruption scandal of the era and a case still cited as the definitive example of cartel capture of the Mexican security state. Convicted on charges including bribery, obstruction and the misuse of army weapons, he received a sentence of 31 years and 10 months, and in 2007 a further 40-year term and a large fine. He died in custody on 19 December 2013.","notable":["Commanded the Fifth Military Region in Guadalajara before heading Mexico's anti-drug agency","Appointed drug czar in December 1996 and praised by US officials as incorruptible","Arrested 18 February 1997 for taking payments from Amado Carrillo Fuentes of the Juárez Cartel","Sentenced to 31 years and 10 months, with an additional 40-year term imposed in 2007","Died of brain cancer at the Central Military Hospital in Mexico City on 19 December 2013"],"state":"CMX","lat":19.4326,"lng":-99.1332,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jes%C3%BAs_Guti%C3%A9rrez_Rebollo","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amado_Carrillo_Fuentes","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ju%C3%A1rez_Cartel"],"verified":false},{"id":"juan_jesus_posadas_ocampo","name":"Juan Jesús Posadas Ocampo","alias":"","cartels":[],"born":1926,"died":1993,"role":"Cardinal and Archbishop of Guadalajara, shot dead at Guadalajara airport in 1993 in Mexico's most disputed assassination","status_2026":"Assassinated May 1993; no one has ever been convicted of his murder and the official crossfire finding remains contested","bio":"Born 10 November 1926 in Salvatierra, Guanajuato, Juan Jesús Posadas Ocampo was ordained in 1950, consecrated bishop in 1970, and served as Bishop of Tijuana from 1970 to 1982 and of Cuernavaca from 1982 to 1987 before becoming Archbishop of Guadalajara. Pope John Paul II made him a cardinal on 28 June 1991. On 24 May 1993, as he waited in his car in the parking area of Guadalajara's Miguel Hidalgo International Airport to meet the papal nuncio, gunmen opened fire; Posadas Ocampo was hit fourteen times at close range and killed along with six other people, including his driver. The government of Carlos Salinas de Gortari concluded within days that he had been caught in crossfire between Tijuana Cartel gunmen and Joaquín Guzmán's people, and that the assassins had mistaken his black Grand Marquis for Guzmán's. The Catholic Church rejected that account almost immediately, noting the point-blank wounds, the cardinal's distinctive clerical dress and pectoral cross, and the unimpeded departure of the shooters on a commercial flight. Successive investigations, including one reopened after Vicente Fox's 2000 election, have implicated Tijuana Cartel figures and raised the possibility that Posadas was killed for what he knew about links between politicians and traffickers. No one has been convicted of the murder itself.","notable":["Archbishop of Guadalajara from 1987 and cardinal from 28 June 1991","Shot fourteen times at Guadalajara airport on 24 May 1993; six others died with him","The Salinas government blamed crossfire and mistaken identity involving Joaquín Guzmán","The Church rejected that finding, citing point-blank wounds and his visible clerical dress","The case was reopened after 2000 but no one has ever been convicted of his killing"],"state":"JAL","lat":20.6597,"lng":-103.3496,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Jes%C3%BAs_Posadas_Ocampo","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tijuana_Cartel","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joaqu%C3%ADn_%22El_Chapo%22_Guzm%C3%A1n"],"verified":false},{"id":"carlos_salinas_de_gortari","name":"Carlos Salinas de Gortari","alias":"","cartels":[],"born":1948,"died":0,"role":"President of Mexico 1988-1994, whose administration presided over the cartels' consolidation and faced enduring allegations of protection","status_2026":"Alive; never charged with any drug-related offence; a private citizen dividing his time between Mexico and Europe and still a recurring figure in Mexican political controversy","bio":"Born 3 April 1948 in Mexico City, Carlos Salinas de Gortari was a Harvard-trained economist and budget secretary who won the disputed 1988 election and governed until 1994. His presidency privatised state industries, negotiated NAFTA and opened the border to unprecedented commercial traffic — the same corridors and volumes that made cartel logistics vastly harder to police. He is included in this atlas for context rather than as a trafficker: he has never been charged with a drug offence, and no court has established that he directed protection of any cartel. But his sexenio was the period in which the Guadalajara Cartel's successors consolidated into the Tijuana, Juárez, Gulf and Sinaloa organizations, and it produced the era's defining scandals — the May 1993 assassination of Cardinal Posadas Ocampo and the government's contested crossfire explanation, the 1994 killings of PRI candidate Luis Donaldo Colosio and party secretary José Francisco Ruiz Massieu, and the 1996 conviction of his brother Raúl Salinas for the Ruiz Massieu murder, later overturned, alongside Swiss and US investigations into more than 100 million dollars in his brother's foreign accounts that Swiss authorities linked to drug protection payments. US prosecutors in the Juan García Ábrego case also alleged high-level protection. Salinas has consistently denied all wrongdoing.","notable":["President of Mexico from 1 December 1988 to 30 November 1994","Negotiated NAFTA, whose commercial traffic reshaped cross-border smuggling economics","His government produced the contested crossfire finding in the 1993 Posadas Ocampo killing","Brother Raúl Salinas convicted in 1996 over the Ruiz Massieu murder, later overturned; Swiss authorities linked his accounts to drug protection payments","Never charged with a drug offence; has denied all allegations and remains alive in 2026"],"state":"CMX","lat":19.4326,"lng":-99.1332,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carlos_Salinas_de_Gortari","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ra%C3%BAl_Salinas_de_Gortari","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Jes%C3%BAs_Posadas_Ocampo"],"verified":false}];
